---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const log = [
  ['Harvested the source', 'The MyHeritage tree read in full — 164 people, 648 facts with their citations. Five duplicate records found and collapsed; about a hundred people identified as a surname index rather than descent.', 'doc'],
  ['Checked it against the Bundesarchiv', 'All four Leibholz men found in the Gedenkbuch. Jacob was deported on 26 February 1943, not killed in 1945 — and Georg, born at Hammerstein two months after Jacob was born at Schubin, cannot be his brother.', 'doc'],
  ['Read the DNA', 'Derrick carries 0.0% Ashkenazi Jewish where a quarter is required, and 1.4% Germanic where a quarter is required. The paternity of record does not survive it.', 'inf'],
  ['Found her name', 'The 1948 divorce file names her in full: Ivone, born MOTA FIGUEIREDO. "Mota" was never a nickname. Her father was a Figueiredo and her mother a Mota.', 'doc'],
  ['Surveyed Mozambique', 'FamilySearch has never filmed anything genealogical for Mozambique. The Arquivo Histórico de Moçambique holds the civil registers for 1865–1934 and takes requests online — and Ivone was born in 1924.', 'doc'],
  ['Worked the passport registers', 'Porto closed as a dead end: its series begins in 1951. Aveiro gave one candidate, Augusto Pereira de Figueiredo, bound for Lourenço Marques in October 1904.', 'doc'],
  ['Read the 1883 baptism book', 'Fifty-one images, no name index, entry Nº 35. Augusto born 12 August 1883, four grandparents named — and a marginal note giving his death at Amadora in 1954, with the registration number that will decide whether he is Ivone’s father.', 'doc'],
  ['Worked the British DNA cluster', 'Five matches who are first cousins to each other are all second cousins to Derrick. One sits a generation closer — her grandparent was a sibling of his biological father, and her own father was born in Johannesburg. Surnames: Halsey, Parr, Fenton.', 'inf'],
  ['Yad Vashem', 'Samuel appears in the List of Jews murdered in Auschwitz as Samuel ISRAEL Leibholz — which is where the compulsory Nazi middle name on this family’s records comes from. Oskar confirmed on the Theresienstadt list, Georg on the Łódź list.', 'doc'],
  ['Opened the photographs', 'Twenty-two people in the tree carry attached images, and several of the “photographs” are documents nobody had read: Julius Leibke’s 1942 Berlin death register, naming a whole new generation — Abraham Leibke and Christine née Berger — and Henriette Katz’s 1926 newspaper death notice, in which the family itself pairs Trude with Oskar.', 'doc'],
  ['Read the 1956 marriage register', 'The largest of those images, 3,918 × 5,245 px, turned out to be the duplicate original register of Kurt’s second marriage — entry No. 169 of 1956. It records his status as DIVORCEE with an order filed, which confirms the 1951 decree from outside the court; and its parents’ columns, which this form has and the 1948 form did not, are ruled through and empty.', 'doc'],
  ['Wrote to three archives', 'Letters sent 15 September 2026, in Portuguese, to the Arquivo da Defesa Nacional at Paço d\'Arcos, the Arquivo Histórico Militar in Lisbon and the Arquivo Histórico de Moçambique in Maputo, asking whether Henrique was recenseado at Lourenço Marques in 1961 and what his military registration says. The Maputo letter is a different letter: this archive already knew their civil registers stop at 1934, so it does not ask for a birth certificate — it asks whether the recruitment-district records stayed in Mozambique after 1975. Each archive is told what the others have been asked.', 'doc'],
  ['WITHDRAWN: the estate notice that was not Kurt\'s', 'On 14 September this archive announced that it had found Kurt\'s estate notice in Government Gazette 2525 of 26 September 1969. On 15 September the gazette was downloaded and read in full. The word Luwinski does not appear in it once. The estate number 7584/69 on that page belongs to KOTZE, Pieter Johannes of Bethal, and the Sanlam Building address quoted with it belongs to the notice immediately above, ROBBERTZE, Alida Christina. Two adjacent entries in a two-column list, and the archive took the number from one and the address from the other. The notice is not found. Kurt\'s estate is known from the National Archives index and nothing else.', 'disp'],
  ['Found Henrique\'s estate', 'The same free gazette archive that found the hat shop also found the deceased estate of Ivone\'s first son — number 12294/2023, gazetted three times in 2023 as DA ROCHA, HENRIQUE FIGUEIREDO PINTO. A South African estate file contains form J294, which asks for the father\'s full names. The man the family tree can only call "Unknown da Rocha" is named on a printed form in a government office, and the file can be ordered by number.', 'doc'],
  ['Found the shop', 'A page of the Union Government Gazette for 30 October 1942, supplied by the family, names Kurt in a partnership notice. Chasing it found Gazettes.Africa — free, full-text, 54,313 South African issues — and eight Luwinski entries. He bought the Piccadilly Hat Company at 81 Eloff Street on 1 November 1941, ran it with Walter Oppenheimer for exactly a year, took it over alone on 31 October 1942, and was still trading there in 1952. Fifteen blank years between his arrival and his marriage are no longer blank.', 'doc'],
  ['Swept all 5,464 matches by surname', 'The archive\'s oldest outstanding item, closed. The search box on the DNA Matches page takes a name OR an ancestral surname and searches the whole list. LEIBHOLZ: no matches. LUWINSKI: no matches. MyHeritage indexes both as Derrick\'s own ancestral surnames, so the search had real terms and still returned nothing across 5,464 people. The German and Jewish absence is now an exhaustive search rather than a sample of six.', 'doc'],
  ['Withdrew the Portuguese half of it, hours later', 'The same sweep returned 27 for FIGUEIREDO and 13 for MOTA, and this archive called it the best lead on Ivone\'s parentage it had ever had — before opening a single match. Opened, the Figueiredo tag turns out to pair her with a woman born before 1713, and MyHeritage states that the Mota matches share no ancestral surname with Derrick at all. A surname tag on a match card is a string comparison between two trees, not a finding. Withdrawn in public, because that is what the corrections layer is for.', 'disp'],
  ['Tested the Y-branch surnames', 'FamilyTreeDNA Discover names the surname projects on R-S11477 — Ballew, Stafford, Blakely, Byrd, York. All five were searched against Derrick\'s match list. Everything came back between 10 and 28 cM, fourth-to-fifth cousin, the noise floor — exactly what a common ancestor in 200 BCE predicts. A negative worth recording, because it says which tool to reach for next and it is the autosomal one.', 'inf'],
  ['The Y-DNA came back', 'R-S11477 — R1b, branch U106/Z381, the North Sea lineage that peaks in the Netherlands and England, and not one characteristic of Ashkenazi paternal descent. The archive had named this as the test that would break the paternity finding or confirm it. It confirmed it, from an independent direction, and the label stays Inferred because a conclusion does not become Documented by surviving a test.', 'doc'],
  ['The 1939 census', 'Addresses at last. Jacob, Auguste and Siegfried at Meraner Straße 2, Schöneberg. And two corrections: Gertrud Leibke was Oskar’s wife, not Georg’s — and Georg’s wife, Lydia née Perl, died on the same day he did.', 'doc'],
  ['Read two words in column four', 'The 1952 Johannesburg marriage register was opened at full resolution to look for the parents of Mervin Halsey. The 1952 form has no parents\' columns — but it has column (4), Country of Birth, and it says Mervin was born in the CAPE PROVINCE and his bride Cynthia Wear in ENGLAND. A week spent on the surname Halsey had been spent on the wrong half of the couple. Cynthia\'s GRO birth entry (Q2 1931, Hitchin, 3A/1345) names her mother as a CASTLE; the only Wear-Castle marriage in the entire English index is Hitchin, Q4 1914; the 1921 census gives her as Martha Maud Wear, born Baldock, Hertfordshire; and the 1901 and 1911 censuses put the whole Castle household at Baldock — with two children, Hilda b.1902 and Ernest b.1904, that the match\'s own tree never recorded. Two of Derrick\'s closest matches descend from that one couple through two different daughters, 297.5 and 292.7 cM. The Castles were never Norfolk; only the Browns were.', 'doc'],
  ['Cross-matched the cluster, then read the segments', 'Centimorgan totals could not separate two readings of where Derrick sat. The shared SEGMENTS could, because they answer a different question — not how much he shares with each match but which parts of his own genome. Of the 297.5 cM he shares with the closest match, only about 44 fall in regions he also shares with any Castle cousin: one block on chromosome 2. The other 253 lie nowhere near them. Two people who share a single ancestral couple cannot do that. He and she descend from Henry Robert Wear AND Martha Maud Castle together — a couple, not a line — so his father was their son.', 'doc'],
  ['Found the man', 'LEONARD AUBREY ROBERT WEAR, birth registered Hitchin 1917, on the 1921 census at 72 Pix Road, Letchworth, aged four — and then absent from every English record there is. He was in Johannesburg: married Phyllis Agnes Robertson at Rosebank on 7 September 1940, divorced by her in 1951, married again that October, divorced again in 1962. Thirty-one in June 1948, in the right city, married to somebody else. And he is why his sister Cynthia was in Johannesburg to marry there in 1952.', 'inf'],
  ['Closed the sibship', 'The GRO birth index carries the mother\u2019s maiden name from 1911, so the whole family can be listed rather than guessed at. Surname WEAR, mother\u2019s maiden name CASTLE, all of England and Wales: six children, every one at Hitchin — 1917, 1919, 1921, 1931, 1933, 1936 — of whom two were sons. In June 1948 one was thirty-one and the other was eleven.', 'doc'],
  ['Then the whole family turned up in Johannesburg', 'Henry Robert Wear died there on 11 September 1969, aged 80, and is buried at West Park; Martha Maud died there on 16 March 1986, aged 90; Bruce Wallace married at Kuruman in 1959. The household this archive had reconstructed in a Hertfordshire market town had picked itself up and moved to the city Derrick was born in.', 'doc'],
  ['Somebody else had joined him to a Castle in 2018', 'A seven-person MyHeritage tree, kept by a South African family who know nothing of Baldock or Letchworth, gives Leonard Wear b.1917 UK d.1973 a mother called \u201cMuad Wear, born Castle\u201d. Muad is Maud. No record anywhere else gives him parents — FamilySearch\u2019s own profile has none to this day. And its profile page labels him \u201cFather (of site manager)\u201d, which identifies the masked daughter as the woman who built it.', 'doc'],
  ['Swept a source that indexes women by maiden name', 'gendatabase.com, given by the family. It answered in four seconds a question that had taken a day: EDWINA ANN WEAR, the candidate half-sister, was born EDWINA ANN GREEN and married Bruce Wallace Wear — Leonard\u2019s younger brother — in 1980. A Wear by marriage, no blood relation at all. Every dead end around her had been the answer telling us so. It also produced three Luwinski court files nobody had seen: WLD 1950/1948, the divorce Kurt filed against Ivone within months of marrying her, and WLD 497/1951 and 2368/1951 behind it.', 'doc'],
  ['Rebuilt four cemetery pages a tile at a time, and answered the 1986 question with a no', 'The West Park deep-zoom images were reassembled from their tiles \u2014 377 for the 1986 spread alone \u2014 and magnified to five times. The result: the register DOES NOT NAME who authorised reopening grave 56045 in 1986. Martha Maud\u2019s Remarks column is empty, and the index side of the private-graves folio holds exactly one Wear, her husband. A second interment into an existing private grave generated no grave transaction, so nobody signed for it. The record was never made, which is a different answer from not having found it, and the row closes with the reason attached.', 'inf'],
  ['CORRECTED: the grave folio is W 8, not W58', 'Published on 15 September as folio W58. The pencilled head-margin number, re-cropped and read on its own at 4.3 times, is a looped W and a single 8 \u2014 the flourish on the W had been read as a 5. Three records agree: the purchaser page\u2019s own margin, the index side of the same folio, and the burial register\u2019s cross-reference \u201cP. Fol. W.8.\u201d Nothing else in the entry changes \u2014 not the purchaser, not the address, not the sixty pounds. But a folio number is how the next person orders the page, and published wrong it sends them to the wrong sheet.', 'disp'],
  ['Got the parents\u2019 own address, and demoted a guess', 'Burial register folio W 56 has an address column, and row 4 is Henry Robert Wear: died 11 September 1969 of bronchopneumonia, aged 80, at the General Hospital, Johannesburg; usual residence 5 LIVINGSTONE CT., 4TH ST., PARKHURST. The first address ever documented for either parent in South Africa. It also bracketed an inference this archive had been leaning on: 64 Greenfield Road, Greenside was never documented as their house \u2014 it was reasoned from an unmarried daughter of twenty-one. The parents are now documented in Parkhurst in 1969 and the widow still in Parkhurst in 1986. Greenside survives as a guess, and now says so.', 'doc'],
  ['Read a dead hostname as a dead service, and was wrong', 'On 16 September the National Archives search system stopped resolving and this archive published that NAAIRS was down. The hostname really had died. THE SERVICE HAD NOT — it had moved to www.national.archsrch.gov.za under a new path, and the portal page the family opened gave the new address in one click. A paragraph telling the Reading Room supervisor that her own system was down was cut out of a letter minutes before it was sent. A URL that does not resolve is a fact about a URL.', 'disp'],
  ['Published two zeroes that were not zeroes', 'The spelling sweep reported LAWINSKI and LUBINSKI as returning nothing in the archives index. Both return three records each. The page now carries the correction in place rather than a silent edit, because NOTHING FOUND and FOUND, AND NOT OURS are different claims and only one of them is a negative.', 'disp'],
  ['Claimed DNA above George William Castle, and withdrew it inside the hour', 'Two match holders carry the surname GIDDINS, 43.36 and 62.89 cM, both in cluster 12 — and James Giddins of Norton End had just been proved as Derrick\'s three-times-great-grandfather. The arithmetic fitted a fourth cousin exactly. Three paragraphs were written calling it the first DNA to touch the Castle line above George William. THEN THE TREE WAS OPENED. Its George Giddins was born on 17 July 1846 at WITHYBROOK, WARWICKSHIRE, father Richard — a different family in a different county. The matches are real and the surname is not the reason for them. The arithmetic was sound and the premise was never checked, and checking it took four minutes.', 'disp'],
  ['Settled WALLIS against WALLS, six to nothing', 'This archive had used both spellings and asserted neither, which is a contradiction to leave lying on a page for weeks. The GRO birth index can be searched on the MOTHER\'S maiden name: Castle births at Hitchin return SIX children under WALLIS — 1884, 1887, 1889, 1892, 1901, 1904 — and none at all under Walls. That is a registrar writing her maiden name down six times across twenty years, against one census in which an enumerator heard her widowed father as Joseph Walls. Walls is withdrawn.', 'doc'],
  ['Reversed the emigration and had it backwards in public first', 'The published account had the father go out first and the son follow. LEONARD SAILED ON 12 MARCH 1937, two years before either parent — a man of twenty going alone, not a family sending for its children. Henry Robert followed on 5 April 1939 and Martha Maud on 10 August with the four youngest, three weeks before the declaration of war.', 'disp'],
  ['A line of Fraktur, and at the end of it a photograph', 'The 1940 Berlin address book has \u201cWilhelm, FLEISCHERMEISTER, Neuk\u00f6lln, LENAUSTRASSE 25\u201d \u2014 an entry this archive nearly missed, because only the first name of a run spells the surname and the rest hang off a dash. Mapping the Lives has ERNA N\u00c9E HAMMER and a son, HEINZ PAUL HUGO, born 1922, at that same address, both dead before the war ended. And Yad Vashem has his Page of Testimony: \u201cHe was a BUTCHER. Prior to WWII he lived in Berlin.\u201d Married, spouse Erna. PLACE OF DEATH: AUSCHWITZ, 1943 \u2014 which is why the 1943 volume has no entry for him, and which no directory could ever have said. SUBMITTED BY EVA KHAVA HAMMER OLSHER, NIECE, who also wrote Erna\u2019s. She is a Hammer: the survivor who recorded this household came through the wife\u2019s side. His record lists four documents and TWO OF THEM ARE PHOTOGRAPHS. None of it connects him to Kurt\u2019s family \u2014 Jacob, Samuel and Oskar were born at Schubin and nothing says where Willi was born beyond \u201cGermany\u201d.', 'doc'],
  ['Raised as new, for the third time in a day, something the archive already had', 'A row was written this morning saying the Chevrah Kadisha was \u201cthe one cemetery this archive ruled out\u201d and that one enquiry would give Kurt a plot and a date. IT HAS ALREADY BEEN CONSULTED. It is the source of the FIRST correction in corrections.json: \u201cKurt Israel Luwinski, died \u2014 was Dec 15 1969, now 15 DECEMBER 1968. Johannesburg Chevrah Kadisha burial register: died 15.12.1968, buried 16.12.1968, WESTPARK BLOCK L GRAVE 211, Yahrzeit 24 Kislev 5729, which is itself 15 December 1968.\u201d The date, the plot and a Hebrew cross-check, all held. The discrepancy the row raised was settled before it was raised. AND THE PATTERN IS WORTH NAMING: three times today I have presented as new something already in this archive \u2014 because I have been reading the work list and the page text and NOT corrections.json, which is where the hardest-won facts live.', 'disp'],
  ['Kurt lies in the same ground as the Wears', 'Kept from the struck row because it deserves not to be lost with it. The Chevrah Kadisha register puts Kurt at WEST PARK, BLOCK L, GRAVE 211, in the Jewish section, buried 16 December 1968. Henry Robert Wear is at West Park in GRAVE 56045, SECTION E.C., bought by his son on 13 September 1969, and Martha Maud was laid in the same grave in 1986. The man who married Derrick\u2019s mother and the parents of the man who fathered him are in one cemetery, a few hundred yards apart, and neither family ever knew the other existed.', 'inf'],
  ['Added the de Witt correction, and did not collapse the duplicate', 'Ivone\u2019s first husband now has a date of death in the register \u2014 2 April 1963 at Sasolburg \u2014 carried as a was/now correction with its reasoning attached, because the source is the tree\u2019s own duplicate and not an outside record. The duplicate itself gets a second correction saying it is not a second person. WHAT IT DID NOT GET IS COLLAPSING. The DUPLICATES map in build-register.py takes records only where the pair shares AN IDENTICAL BIRTH AND DEATH DATE, and says in its own comment that those are not guesses. This pair does not meet that bar \u2014 Johannesburg against Pretoria, one birth \u201ccirca\u201d, only one death recorded. Widening a rule quietly to fit a case is how the two Emma Hawkinses happened. Both records stay visible with the correction on them.', 'doc'],
  ['Audited every citation in the tree, and the first pass of the audit was wrong', 'Fifty-nine cited facts across three hosts. AROLSEN: seven citations, ALL SEVEN POINTING AT ONE DOCUMENT, and only one of the seven is supported by it. FAMILYSEARCH: forty-six, and only TWO are wrong. NAAIRS: six, none verifiable \u2014 they are URLs with session tokens and the service has not answered since 16 September. THE FIRST PASS SAID THIRTY-THREE FAMILYSEARCH CITATIONS WERE WRONG. It was counting parish registers: the record is the BAPTISM or the BURIAL and the tree correctly holds the birth or the death \u2014 Stanilaus born 27 September 1810 and baptised 14 October, Valentin dead on 12 July 1878 and buried on the 15th. Twenty-five of the thirty-three were that. The two that are real are one error made twice: Ivone\u2019s and Johannes de Witt\u2019s marriage of 2 FEBRUARY 1948 both cite the record of KURT and Ivone\u2019s marriage of 19 JUNE 1948. The right record was already in hand from three days ago.', 'disp'],
  ['A man the tree has twice, and the duplicate was carrying his death', 'The same audit checked 164 people by arithmetic alone and found three impossibilities. Two are Martha Schuenke, dead in 1930 with sons born 1933 and 1940. THE THIRD PAYS FOR THE WHOLE EXERCISE. JOHANNES ANTONIE OELOFSE DE WITT, born December 1923 at Johannesburg, has a SON OF THE SAME NAME born CIRCA 1923 AT PRETORIA \u2014 a father aged nought \u2014 and both men die at SASOLBURG. They are one man. And the duplicate carries a fact the original lacks: DEATH 2 APRIL 1963. So IVONE\u2019S FIRST HUSBAND DIED AT SASOLBURG ON 2 APRIL 1963, AGED THIRTY-NINE, and the date was sitting inside a duplicate nobody had looked at. The split also invented a phantom wife and a spurious half-sister.', 'doc'],
  ['A citation that cannot be falsified from its own text is not much of a citation', 'The reason the FamilySearch citations are 44 out of 46 sound and the Arolsen ones are 1 out of 7 is not that one archive is better than the other. It is that a FamilySearch citation carries the RECORD\u2019S OWN DESCRIPTION in its text \u2014 \u201cEntry for X and Y, 19 Jun 1948\u201d \u2014 so a wrong one announces itself without anything being opened. An Arolsen citation is a bare URL. There is nothing in it to check against, which is exactly why six wrong ones survived in a tree that is otherwise careful. The audit is now a tool, tools/audit-tree.py, so it can be re-run rather than remembered.', 'inf'],
  ['An Arolsen index entry is a pointer, not a fact', 'Four documents were opened with the consent and TWO OF THEM SHOW THE TREE HAS MISATTACHED A RECORD, both in the same shape. Alfred, born 9.9.1917, is made a father of children born in 1933 and 1940; his card says Polish, Catholic, farm labourer, SINGLE, Sachsenhausen, dead in July 1942 at twenty-four. Felix, born 19.2.1923, is given a birthplace of GRAFENAU, GERMANY; his document gives no birthplace at all \u2014 it is a Form 11 return of the LANDRAT GRAFENAU dated 19 AUGUST 1946, an Allied listing of foreigners in the American Zone, and its nationality field reads RUSSLAND. The place of registration has become the place of birth. THE RULE FOLLOWS, AND THIS ARCHIVE SHOULD APPLY IT TO ITSELF FIRST: an index entry gives a name, a date of birth and a DocID, and everything else is on the document. The 191 Leibholz index entries recorded here without opening anything are pointers, and the file now says so.', 'disp'],
  ['The man the tree calls a father died at Sachsenhausen, single, at twenty-four', 'The tree makes ALFRED LUWINSKI, born 9 September 1917, the father of Manfred and Joachim \u2014 and cites an Arolsen record as its source. That record was opened. It is a registration card of the Berlin Office for the Registration of War Victims, and every field contradicts the tree: Geburtsort a POLISH place, Beruf LANDWIRT, religion KATH., Nationalit\u00e4t POLN., Familienstand LEDIG \u2014 SINGLE \u2014 inhaftiert SACHSENHAUSEN, and \u201c\u2020 11.7.42 15:20, Herz- und Kreislaufschw\u00e4che\u201d, which is the standard camp formula and is not a cause of death. The reverse gives his home: PRZASNYSZ, PI\u0141SUDSKIEGO 117. A Polish Catholic farm labourer who never married and died at twenty-four. HE IS NOT THE FATHER OF ANYBODY BORN IN 1933. The tree cites this exact card for Manfred\u2019s 1933 birth and for Martha Schuenke\u2019s 1930 death, and it contains neither fact and neither person. Manfred and Joachim\u2019s father is unknown; the only candidate left is the Alfred Luwinski who was a B\u00dcGLER and a head of household at Putbusser Stra\u00dfe 24 in 1933.', 'disp'],
  ['A branch of this family flew from Berlin to Australia in 1965', 'The consent declaration was ticked and the emigration file opened. It is an INTERGOVERNMENTAL COMMITTEE FOR EUROPEAN MIGRATION NOMINAL ROLL, headed MELBOURNE, marked \u201cAUSTRALIAN GOVERNMENT FINANCIALLY ASSISTED\u201d, stamped SEPTEMBER 1965, filed under \u201cEmigrations \u2014 transport by airplane\u201d. Four lines of it are one family: MANFRED LUWINSKI, born 14.11.33, aged 32, of BERLIN, occupation MODELL FITTER; HELGA, aged 31; and two children aged ten and three, who are living and are not named here. Forwarding address: c/o Mr H. MARQUARDT, Canberra ACT \u2014 and the tree carries Helga as HELGA ERNA MARQUARDT. Her family was already in Australia and brought them out. THE BERLIN DIRECTORY LINES UP EXACTLY: Manfred is a SCHLOSSER at Egellsstra\u00dfe 16, Tegel, in 1957, 1959 and 1961, and vanishes after 1961. Schlosser and model fitter are the same trade. He vanishes because he left the country. And the tree already knew where he ended up without knowing how: MANFRED JOACHIM LUWINSKI DIED AT BRISBANE IN 2012.', 'doc'],
  ['A generation in the tree that cannot be true as written', 'Reading the tree against the documents turned up a fault in the tree. It makes ALFRED LUWINSKI, born 9 September 1917, the father of Manfred born 1933 and Joachim born 1940 \u2014 and gives his wife, Martha Schuenke, a death in 1930. A father of sixteen, and a mother three years dead at the first birth. The 1933 Berlin directory meets the same problem from the other side: its Alfred Luwinski is a B\u00dcGLER AND A HEAD OF HOUSEHOLD, which fits a man old enough to father a child that year and not a boy of sixteen. Three readings and none chosen. WHAT WOULD SETTLE IT IS IN QUEENSLAND: Manfred died at Brisbane in 2012, and an Australian death certificate names the deceased\u2019s parents.', 'disp'],
  ['Ruled out the one cemetery Kurt is actually in', 'When this archive swept the Johannesburg municipal cemetery registers it stated its bounds carefully, including that the sweep did not cover the Chevrah Kadisha, \u201ca separate database \u2014 a Wear would have no business in a Jewish cemetery, but the exclusion is stated rather than assumed\u201d. That is right about the Wears and WRONG ABOUT KURT. The family tree gives his burial as CHEVRA KADISHA, JOHANNESBURG. He was registered as a Jew in Germany and carried the imposed name out of it. The burial society keeps its own database, one enquiry would give a plot and usually whoever arranged the burial \u2014 and it may settle a discrepancy this archive is carrying, because the work list says he died on 15 December 1968 and the tree says 15 December 1969.', 'disp'],
  ['Arolsen holds Kurt himself, and six more of his family', 'Searching the Arolsen Archives for the whole surname returns NINE records, and seven of them match this archive\u2019s own register by exact date of birth. KURT IS ONE OF THEM \u2014 \u201cLUWINSKI, Kurt, 05/11/1912\u201d, DocID 11214358, \u201c1 Incarceration Documents / 1.2.1 DEPORTATIONS AND TRANSPORTS / Part 24: Folder MFN01335\u201d. That is the same DocID already held for Jacob and Auguste, so the three of them are in one folder of five hundred documents, and his index date of birth matches the register to the day. The archive knew the DocID; it did not know Kurt was indexed under his own name inside it. The others sit in four different classes: FELIX in the Allied registration of foreigners and persecutees; MANFRED AND HELGA SHARING A SINGLE DocID in \u201c3.1.3 Emigrations \u2014 correspondence and nominal rolls\u201d; ALFRED in post-war evaluations. Two of the nine are people flagged living in the family tree and are not named here. EVERYTHING ABOVE IS THE INDEX ENTRY: every document behind it needs a consent declaration ticked, and this archive does not tick declarations on anybody\u2019s behalf.', 'doc'],
  ['Two leads closed by absence, and only one of the absences means anything', 'OSKAR LUWINSKI, the master tailor of Steglitzer Stra\u00dfe 7, is in neither Arolsen nor Mapping the Lives. He exists in ONE LINE of ONE directory and in no record of Nazi persecution at all \u2014 consistent with a man who was not persecuted, and one absence in two archives proves nothing. ANTON LUWINSKI of Deuthen is not in Arolsen either, AND THAT ADDS NOTHING: his own 1939 card already reads \u201crace NNNN, basis of persecution unknown\u201d, so a persecution archive should not hold him, and it does not. The two sources agree and neither takes him further. He stops being a separate lead and becomes part of the Ermland case \u2014 a Luwinski born twenty miles from Lizzy\u2019s village, waiting on the same undigitised films.', 'inf'],
  ['Named the film this archive has been reading for a week without naming it', 'Twice now a West Park register page has been reached by opening images by hand, and neither time was the film written down \u2014 so every grave question started from nothing. Behind the indexed collection sits catalogue record olib:4148464, TWO HUNDRED AND TEN FILMS of Johannesburg cemetery registers, twelve of them West Park private graves by year range. Leonard bought grave 56045 on 13 September 1969, so folio W 8 is in FILM 107638608, \u201cWPC Private Graves Registers 1963\u20131970\u201d, 488 images, and nowhere else. Opened, it reads alphabetically by purchaser surname \u2014 image 401 is the T section, Theunissen, Tyson, Terblanche, Taljaard, Taylor \u2014 which puts the F folios around 180 to 200. Folio F 3/8 itself was NOT read: the viewer kept reverting to its thumbnail grid and the hunt was abandoned rather than padded out. The film number is worth more than the folio, because the folio may not even be Wear\u2019s.', 'inf'],
  ['Found Lizzy\u2019s parish, and found that eight of its nine films are not online', 'A village means a parish and a parish means a register. The FamilySearch catalogue names it in its own words: \u201cParish register of baptisms and deaths for GROSS B\u00d6SSAU, Ostpreu\u00dfen, now Biesowo, Olsztyn, Poland. Also includes Kunzkeim, SAUERBAUM, Willims and Kleisak (Kreis R\u00f6ssel)\u201d \u2014 1690 to 1897, on nine films. EIGHT OF THE NINE ARE NOT DIGITISED. The two that matter both answer \u201cfilm number not found\u201d while signed in: 7948905, TAUFEN 1793\u20131890, which would hold Joachim Luwinski\u2019s 1865 baptism and NAME HIS PARENTS; and 7769256, GEBURTEN (KOPIE) 1881\u20131958, which would hold Lizzy\u2019s birth. The one film that does open was checked to the frame: at image 591 the camera photographed the title board, \u201cGROSS-B\u00d6SSAU / KRS.R\u00d6SSEL / 1798-1847 / \u2020\u201d. Right parish, wrong years.', 'inf'],
  ['Seventy-one Luwinskis in the world and not one of them East Prussian', 'With the sign-in restored, the surname was matched exactly across all of FamilySearch: 71 records. Twenty-two West Prussia, thirteen Posen, two Silesia, and the rest American immigration and the two South African marriages. NONE IN EAST PRUSSIA \u2014 where Lizzy was born. AND THAT IS A FACT ABOUT THE INDEX, NOT THE FAMILY: the Ermland registers are held as catalogued film and no name index reaches them, and the full-text instrument that reads unindexed images returns, for Luwinski, exactly what it returned three days ago \u2014 Milwaukee and Chicago naturalisations, all American. One more label to distrust: the viewer files this Catholic parish under \u201cGermany, LUTHERAN Baptisms, Marriages and Burials\u201d. Anyone trusting it would conclude these were the wrong registers and go home.', 'disp'],
  ['Lizzy\u2019s own census card, and it answers three questions at once', 'AUGUSTE LEIBHOLZ N\u00c9E LUWINSKI, born 28.08.1893 in SAUERBAUM / R\u00d6SSEL / OSTPREUSSEN \u2014 and this archive already knew that village, because Johanna Thimm OF SAUERBAUM is Kurt\u2019s great-grandmother and was used as the East Prussian control when counting Berlin directory pages. Her card records the ancestry of all four grandparents and reads \u201cRACE\u201d: NNNN \u2014 four times nicht-j\u00fcdisch. THE GERMAN STATE CLASSIFIED HER AS NOT JEWISH, ON ITS OWN FORM, IN 1939. And her basis of persecution is given as COLLECTIVE, the term for someone taken as part of a household rather than for their own recorded ancestry \u2014 a mixed marriage, which is the standard reason Jacob was not seized until the Fabrikaktion of 26 February 1943. The same record\u2019s family block gives Jacob a place of death the Gedenkbuch leaves blank: AUSCHWITZ.', 'doc'],
  ['Fixed the wording, and the page had already said it one paragraph further down', 'Correction 5 called Georg \u201ca different Leibholz family\u201d and gave as its reason that Hammerstein is \u201ctwo hundred kilometres\u201d from Schubin. Both are now withdrawn: SALLY LEIBHOLZ, JACOB\u2019S OWN FATHER, WAS BORN AT HAMMERSTEIN on 7 July 1847, so the distance is not evidence of anything and the surname there is not a stranger\u2019s. WHAT RULES OUT BROTHERHOOD IS THE SIXTY-TWO DAYS, not the two hundred kilometres, and the correction now rests on that alone. The Leibholz page carried the same fault \u2014 \u201cThese are two Leibholz families. Ours is the Schubin one\u201d \u2014 ONE PARAGRAPH ABOVE A PANEL THAT SAYS THE OPPOSITE: \u201cthe family had people in both towns\u2026 right surname, RIGHT FAMILY, wrong generation\u201d. The page now says in as many words what it used to claim and that it was wrong.', 'disp'],
  ['And the panel I had not read gave the letter its best line', 'The same panel carries the marriage date: SZUBIN, 24 NOVEMBER 1874. Prussian civil registration began on 1 OCTOBER 1874, so the marriage is SEVEN WEEKS INSIDE IT and must be an ordinary Standesamt entry rather than a Jewish community register. The Bydgoszcz letter drafted an hour earlier had raised that boundary as an open question and asked the archive to check both. It no longer speculates \u2014 it gives the date, says which book the record should be in, and keeps the community register only as a fallback. That is the fourth time today something was raised as new that this archive already held, and the cause has not changed: I read the work list and the top of a page, and the answer was further down it.', 'disp'],
  ['Hammerstein is not a stranger\u2019s town, and a published correction leans on the idea that it is', 'Drafting the Bydgoszcz letter turned up something that cuts against this archive. CORRECTION 5 disputes Georg Leibholz\u2019s brotherhood on the ground that he was born at HAMMERSTEIN, West Prussia, two months and two hundred kilometres from Jacob at SCHUBIN in Posen. The argument treats Hammerstein as a place this family has nothing to do with. IT IS NOT. The front-page chart says the great-grandparents were \u201cHammerstein \u00b7 married Szubin 1874\u201d, and the letter sent to Koszalin on 15 September asked for \u201cStandesamt Hammerstein 1884 \u2014 Georg; AND SALLY, 1847\u201d. SALLY LEIBHOLZ, JACOB\u2019S OWN FATHER, WAS BORN AT HAMMERSTEIN. The arithmetic still rules out brotherhood \u2014 two sons of one mother cannot be born two months apart \u2014 but the geography is gone from the argument. A Leibholz born at Hammerstein in 1884 is not a stranger sharing a surname; he is a Leibholz born in the town Jacob\u2019s father came from, WHICH IS WHAT A COUSIN LOOKS LIKE.', 'disp'],
  ['No East Prussian index exists, which is why the Thimms cannot be found', 'David asked what could be discovered about the Thimms. All 2,500 FamilySearch collections were enumerated. For the Prussian provinces it has indexed church records for POSEN \u2014 11.6 million \u2014 SILESIA, POMERANIA, BRANDENBURG and WEST PRUSSIA. FOR EAST PRUSSIA IT HAS TWO: both K\u00f6nigsberg funeral sermons, both image-only with zero indexed records, ending in 1794 and 1900. There is no East Prussian church index at all. That is also the retrospective explanation for something odd found on Friday \u2014 seventy-one Luwinskis in the whole world and not one East Prussian. IT IS THE INDEX THAT STOPS AT THE PROVINCIAL BORDER, NOT THE FAMILY. And a join the archive already had the pieces for: JOHANNA THIMM died at ZEUTERN in 1955, AUGUSTE died at KRAICHTAL in 1975, AND ZEUTERN IS A DISTRICT OF KRAICHTAL. The page lists them as two places. Mother and daughter died in the same small town twenty years apart.', 'inf'],
  ['Friederike Schmul is not in the church books, and she would not be', 'The Posen collection holds thirty-three people with that exact surname and all thirty-three were read. No Friederike, no Szubin 1874. What IS there is Jewish \u2014 Wolf Schmul and Beila Lepotke, Isidor Schmul and Liebchen Katz \u2014 including a RICKE SCHMUL, which is a diminutive of Friederike, born in January 1832 and therefore FIFTY-TWO when Jacob was born in 1884. Right name, right province, wrong generation; written down so nobody else chases her. She is absent because those are CHURCH books and a Jewish marriage is not in one. Prussian civil registration began on 1 OCTOBER 1874, so a Szubin marriage that year is a Judenregister or a STANDESAMT entry \u2014 and either way it is at Bydgoszcz, where this archive already has an acknowledged letter sitting. A Prussian marriage certificate of 1874 names BOTH SETS OF PARENTS, a generation above the top of the chart.', 'doc'],
  ['A Geneteka negative that the control destroyed', 'The Polish Genealogical Society\u2019s index was searched for Leibholz marriages in Kujawsko-Pomorskie, 1860 to 1890. Nothing. Then the same query for KOWALSKI \u2014 the commonest Polish surname there is \u2014 and that returned nothing too. The URL parameters are wrong or the search needs a posted form. THE LEIBHOLZ ZERO MEANS NOTHING, is not recorded as a negative, and Geneteka remains untested.', 'disp'],
  ['The file the consent does not reach, and the one it does', 'T/D 52.971, the tracing case for OSKAR LEIBHOLZ, serves METADATA AND NO IMAGES even with the declaration ticked \u2014 no document list, no DocIDs, no viewer \u2014 and says why: \u201cparts of the file are not displayed for data protection reasons. In such cases, please contact the Arolsen Archives.\u201d The consent opens deportation lists and registration cards and STOPS AT THE CORRESPONDENCE, which is what a T/D file is. One thing the metadata does give: CREATION DATE 1949 TO 2002 \u2014 somebody asked about Oskar in 1949 and the file was still being added to fifty-three years later. A letter is drafted and not sent. BUT A DIFFERENT SERIES IS SERVED WITHOUT RESTRICTION: 6.3.1.1, Tracing Inquiries 1945\u20131946, which returns forty-three topics for this surname.', 'inf'],
  ['A woman in Palestine wrote to Jerusalem in May 1946 asking what had become of them', 'JEWISH AGENCY FOR PALESTINE, SEARCH BUREAU FOR MISSING RELATIVES, Questionnaire No. 2, \u201cLocation of Relatives Abroad\u201d, dated 23 MAY 1946, file 50764. THE PERSON SOUGHT: LEIBHOLZ, WILLI, aged 55, BORN AT STOLP IN POMMERN, last information MARCH 1943 FROM BERLIN, last address BERLIN-NEUK\u00d6LLN, LENAUSTRASSE 25, occupation BUTCHER. With him: ERNA, 48, born HAMMER, wife; HEINZ, 23, son; AND RUTH, 22, DAUGHTER \u2014 a child this archive did not have. THE PERSON WHO ASKED: TABORI-HAMMER born NACHUM, RUTH-TRUDE, of BENEI-BRAQ, Palestine, born 9 September 1899 at Hamburg, formerly of Berlin-Moabit, Turmstra\u00dfe 59, relationship \u201cBROTHER IN LAW AND HIS FAMILY\u201d, signed in her own hand. The Tracing Service answered her through the Jewish Agency on 8 December 1947 and closed the case a fortnight later.', 'doc'],
  ['And it settles where Willi was born, which this archive left open this morning', 'The Page of Testimony said only \u201cGermany\u201d, and the note written this morning said so in as many words. The 1946 form says STOLP IN POMMERN. Jacob, Samuel and Oskar were all born at SCHUBIN IN POSEN \u2014 two hundred and fifty miles and a different province \u2014 SO THE LENAUSTRASSE LEIBHOLZES ARE DOCUMENTED AS NOT KURT\u2019S FAMILY rather than merely unproven, and this morning\u2019s caution turns out to have been the right one. Two Hammers survived and both went looking: Ruth-Trude in 1946, and Eva Khava Hammer Olsher, who wrote Willi\u2019s and Erna\u2019s Pages of Testimony later \u2014 BOTH THROUGH ERNA\u2019S FAMILY. The honest disappointment is that the row wanted a survivor on KURT\u2019S side, and the file that would give one is the closed one.', 'doc'],
  ['Read corrections.json properly, and it caught two more things', 'After raising the Chevrah Kadisha as new when it was already the first entry in the file, the whole of corrections.json was read against the eighty-four open rows. TWO HITS. First, a row plans to have MORRIS ISAAC SALKOW\u2019S CAPE TOWN ESTATE \u201cride along\u201d with a Cape Town request \u2014 and correction 3 says he died and was buried in JOHANNESBURG, Westpark block U grave 2469, on 8 November 1991. A better candidate for the Cape file surfaced in Friday\u2019s FamilySearch sweep: ISAAC MORRIS SALKOW, born 1897 in Lithuania, DIED IN THE CAPE PROVINCE ON 10 OCTOBER 1938, probate 1938\u201339. The forenames are reversed and the deaths are fifty-three years apart. Nobody should order that file as Ivone\u2019s husband\u2019s until the reference is checked against a date. Second, the row blocked on the Arolsen consent is now openable, and it named its own next step: a TRACING CASE FOR OSKAR, T/D 52.971, fourteen documents \u2014 and a tracing file names the person who asked.', 'inf'],
  ['Called Jacob a stepfather, and the archive\u2019s own record calls him the father', 'This morning\u2019s entry reasoned that because Kurt was born in 1912 and Lizzy married Jacob in 1924, Kurt must be \u201cher son by another man\u201d and Jacob his stepfather. THAT IS AN ASSUMPTION AND THE SOURCE SAYS OTHERWISE: the family tree records JACOB LEIBHOLZ AS KURT\u2019S FATHER, and work-list row 152 states the archive\u2019s own position in the same terms. A CHILD BORN TWELVE YEARS BEFORE HIS PARENTS MARRY IS ILLEGITIMATE, NOT FATHERLESS \u2014 and that is precisely why he carried his mother\u2019s surname while his younger brother was registered a Leibholz. Auguste was eighteen in May 1912 and Jacob twenty-seven. THE POINT SURVIVES AND IMPROVES: if Lizzy was NNNN then Kurt\u2019s classification as a Jew came from his father, and the father has a name. Under the 1935 laws a child of one Jewish and one non-Jewish parent was a MISCHLING and did not carry the imposed name; somebody counted as a Jew did. Kurt was counted a Jew \u2014 a question with a documentable answer rather than a loose end.', 'disp'],
  ['Called it a persecution database, and the card said otherwise', 'An hour after writing that Anton Luwinski was \u201cin a persecution database\u201d, his record was opened. \u201cRace\u201d: NNNN. \u201cBasis of persecution: unknown.\u201d Mapping the Lives indexes surviving 1939 census supplementary cards, and being in it is not evidence of persecution. What the record does give is a birthplace: DEUTHEN, KREIS ALLENSTEIN \u2014 in the Ermland, about twenty miles from Sauerbaum where Lizzy was born. Two Luwinskis from two villages in the same small Catholic corner of East Prussia. A better reason to look than the one that was claimed, and still not a relationship.', 'disp'],
  ['Laid the directory against the memorial database, household by household', 'The Berlin address book gives Leibholz HOUSEHOLDERS with trades and street numbers. Mapping the Lives, built on the 1939 census card, gives PEOPLE with dates of birth and death at addresses \u2014 114 of them under this surname. Matched on STREET AND HOUSE NUMBER rather than on name, they line up. \u201cA. Israel, Fleischermeister, Levetzowstra\u00dfe 13\u201d is ALFRED LEIBHOLZ, born 04.12.1887, DIED 8 AUGUST 1942 \u2014 which is why the 1942 volume has no entry for him. And he is not alone at that address: ELSE n\u00e9e SEELIG, died August 1943; KURT, born 1927; LIESELOTTE, born 1923, died December 1943; MARIANNE, born 1921. A master butcher, his wife and three children in one back-building flat. The Weinstra\u00dfe traveller who became a building labourer had a wife, JENNY n\u00e9e ROSENBERG, dead before the war ended. The Lenaustra\u00dfe butcher lost ERNA and a son of twenty.', 'doc'],
  ['And the control that stops the whole reading running away with itself', 'HEINRICH LEIBHOLZ, Verwaltungsbeamter, Leinestra\u00dfe 32, Neuk\u00f6lln, is in the directory every year to 1942 and gone from 1943. HE DIED ON 9 AUGUST 1948. He survived the war. So a Leibholz vanishing from the Berliner Adressbuch does not mean a death, and the series read off the page images today \u2014 seventeen, eleven, ten, nine, six, four \u2014 cannot be read as a casualty count. It never claimed to be. Now there is a named man proving why it must not be. One more caution from the same table: ARTHUR LEIBHOLZ, Sanit\u00e4tsrat, is printed in the 1938 volume and had died in NOVEMBER 1937. These books are compiled a year ahead and carry the dead for a year.', 'disp'],
  ['Two Luwinskis in the whole memorial database, and one of them is new', 'Searching it for LUWINSKI returns exactly two people. One is Lizzy \u2014 \u201cLeibholz, Auguste n\u00e9e Luwinski, *28.08.1893, Meraner Str. 2\u201d. The other is ANTON LUWINSKI, BORN 16 OCTOBER 1919, at SCHINDEKOPSTRASSE 7, LEHRWERKSTATT OSTPREUSSISCHES BAUGEWERBE, K\u00d6NIGSBERG \u2014 the training workshop of the East Prussian building trade. This archive\u2019s East Prussia page is built on nineteenth-century parish entries; Anton is the first twentieth-century Luwinski found in the province, and he is in a persecution database. Neither Oskar the master tailor nor Alfred the presser is there at all. Nothing connects Anton to Lizzy but a surname and a province, which is a reason to look and not a relationship.', 'inf'],
  ['A search that turns Luwinski into L\u00f6winsky, caught by opening the page', 'Sweeping the Berlin householder section for LUWINSKI across 1900 to 1943 returned the name in two years: 1933 and 1909. A 1909 householder would be Lizzy\u2019s father\u2019s generation, which this archive has never had \u2014 and a work-list row was written about him. THEN THE PAGE WAS OPENED. It runs \u201cL\u00d6WENTHAL \u2014 1639 \u2014 L\u00d6WISOHN\u201d and there is no Luwinski on it at all: it is Semu Lowinski, Dr Julius Lowinskis, Willy Lowinsky Ingenieur G.m.b.H., R. Lowinsky Nachfolger Dampf-Waschanstalt. The search fuzzes LU to LO and \u00d6. Four minutes to check, and it would have taken a day to unpick.', 'disp'],
  ['Turning that doubt on a finding already published, and finding a master tailor', 'If the 1909 hit was a mirage, the 1933 one had to be re-verified too. IT HOLDS \u2014 page 1671 runs LUTZ to LUX, the right alphabetical place, and the line reads \u201cLuwinski Alfred B\u00fcgler N31 Putbusser Str 24\u201d. But directly beneath it, on the dash, is a name the text layer had hidden: \u201c\u2014OSKAR SCHNEIDERMSTR W35 STEGLITZER STR 7\u201d. OSKAR LUWINSKI, A MASTER TAILOR, with his own business. Lost to the same convention that hid four Leibholz households this morning, twice in one day. There were TWO Luwinski households in Berlin in the only year the name appears at all \u2014 A PRESSER AND A MASTER TAILOR, BOTH IN THE GARMENT TRADE. Lizzy Luwinski was a dressmaker and milliner.', 'doc'],
  ['Kurt\u2019s mother, perhaps, in Charlottenburg in 1957', 'The post-war Berlin volumes are not in the series this archive had been querying \u2014 that one stops at 1943 \u2014 but they exist under other identifiers, and they are set in Antiqua, so the text simply reads. The 1957 Namenteil, page 518: \u201cFricke Auguste Rentn Chlb 1 Kais-Friedr-Str 79\u201d. AUGUSTE FRICKE, A PENSIONER, KAISER-FRIEDRICH-STRASSE 79, CHARLOTTENBURG. Lizzy was born in August 1893 and died in June 1975, so she was sixty-four in 1957 and \u201cRentnerin\u201d is exactly right; she is the only Auguste Fricke in the Berlin name sections of 1955 to 1980. A CANDIDATE AND NOT A FINDING: Fricke is a common North German surname and one name in one directory is how the Bloom mariner went wrong. Her death registration of 22 June 1975 would carry an address to compare.', 'inf'],
  ['Separated the two Kurts at last', 'Every build prints the same complaint: \u201cKURT LUWINSKI matches more than one register entry and is deliberately NOT LINKED\u201d \u2014 Kurt Israel of Johannesburg who died in 1968, and a Kurt who died in 1995. THE SECOND KURT IS NOW PLACED. The 1964 Berlin name section gives \u201cLuwinski Kurt Postfacharb -21 Stephan[str]\u201d \u2014 A POST OFFICE WORKER IN MOABIT \u2014 and the 1970\u201371 telephone book gives him a number. Two more of the family are there with him: MANFRED, a fitter, at Egellsstra\u00dfe 16 in Tegel across 1957, 1959 and 1961, and JOACHIM, a stove-fitter, in Ramlerstra\u00dfe in 1967. The register already holds Manfred Joachim Luwinski born 1933 and Joachim Luwinski born 1940. The two Kurts are now separated by city, trade and decade instead of by a death date alone.', 'doc'],
  ['Published that Kurt lived at the hat shop, and it was the same fact twice', 'This morning\u2019s entry said the wholesale hatter\u2019s address matched one of KURT\u2019S four Berlin addresses, and that a second matched Oskar\u2019s. IT IS CIRCULAR AND IT IS WITHDRAWN. Those four addresses are not Kurt\u2019s. They are the four Leibholz households in the CENSUS OF 17 MAY 1939 \u2014 Jacob, Auguste and Siegfried at Meraner Stra\u00dfe 2; Samuel at Kommandantenstr. 29; Oskar and Gertrud at Waghäuseler Str. 9/10; Georg and Lydia at Michaelkirchstr. 26 \u2014 carried on the atlas under a loosely worded pin. Saying the directory agreed with them was saying the same thing twice, and it went into a commit message before it was checked. Third correction of the day.', 'disp'],
  ['What the directory actually adds: four men, four trades', 'The census gave four addresses and no occupations. The 1938 and 1939 pages give the occupations. JACOB traded as JAQUES \u2014 \u201cJaques Kaufm Sch\u00f6nebg Meraner Str 2\u201d \u2014 and printed directly beneath him is \u201cJaques Leibholz & Co, KONFEKTION, W8 Kronenstra\u00dfe 59.60\u201d, so the ready-made clothing firm this archive has been watching since 1924 IS HIS. SAMUEL sold hats wholesale. OSKAR was a Kaufmann. And GEORG RAN A LAUNDRY at Michaelkirchstra\u00dfe 26. None of that disturbs the argument that Georg was not their brother \u2014 he was born at Hammerstein, they at Schubin \u2014 it just gives four men their working lives.', 'doc'],
  ['Read the whole surname off the page images, year by year, and found the law doing its work', 'The text layer could not read these pages and this archive said so. The IIIF page images can. Leibholz householders in the Berlin directory: SEVENTEEN in 1938, ELEVEN in 1939, TEN in 1940, NINE in 1941, SIX in 1942, FOUR in 1943. But the number is not the finding. THE NAMES ARE. In 1940 Samuel is no longer Samuel \u2014 the entry reads simply \u201cIsrael, H\u00fcte, Kommandantenstr 29\u201d. In 1941 two more men carry it, and both have lost their trade in a single year: A. ISRAEL LEIBHOLZ of Levetzowstra\u00dfe 13 from FLEISCHERMEISTER to ARBEITER, WILHELM ISRAEL LEIBHOLZ of Weinstra\u00dfe 6 from VERTRETER to BAUARBEITER. IN 1942 EVERY NAME CARRYING \u201cISRAEL\u201d IS GONE. The four who survive to 1943 \u2014 a police official, a nurse, a clerk, a bank official \u2014 are exactly the four who never carried it. The directory marks them, and then loses them.', 'doc'],
  ['Jaques Leibholz, Meraner Stra\u00dfe 2, last seen in the 1939 volume', 'Item 7 of the Berlin list asked for Jacob at Meraner Stra\u00dfe 2 year by year, on the reasoning that \u201cthe year he stops appearing is the year the record of his life in that city stops\u201d. He is in the 1938 volume and the 1939 volume and NO VOLUME AFTER. The 1936 street section lists the tenants of Meraner Stra\u00dfe 1 to 5 and no Leibholz is among them, so the household arrived between 1936 and 1938. He was deported from Berlin on 26 February 1943; the directory loses him three volumes before that. One entry away from him, in 1940 only, is \u201cAuguste, WITWE, Sch\u00f6neberg, Badensche Stra\u00dfe 9\u201d \u2014 and the archive will not join those two up, because Jacob was alive until 1943 and a widow in 1940 cannot be his wife unless something happened that nobody here knows about.', 'inf'],
  ['Measured the wrong thing twice in one day, on one page', 'The morning\u2019s correction was that a page count had mixed four sections of the book. The afternoon\u2019s is worse and on the same page: IN A GERMAN DIRECTORY ONLY THE FIRST ENTRY OF A RUN SPELLS THE SURNAME, and every entry after it begins with a dash. Searching the text for \u201cLeibholz\u201d finds one line in ten. The file published at lunchtime said that from 1939 nothing was left under the name but a printer\u2019s cross-reference. FOLLOWED PROPERLY, THE 1940 VOLUME HOLDS ELEVEN LEIBHOLZ HOUSEHOLDERS \u2014 a master butcher, a police official, a widow, a pensioner, a clerk, a bank official, the hat wholesaler and two Wilhelms. What saved the published version was that it had refused to state the disappearance as a finding. The refusal was right; the description underneath it was wrong.', 'disp'],
  ['One page number, four grooms, and two brick walls on the same certificate', 'SARAH BLOOM, marriage registered Q3 1842, STEPNEY, VOLUME 2 PAGE 430 \u2014 and the other names printed on that page are WILLIAM WEARE, Thomas Moyes, William George Smith and James Wicks. Before 1912 the index does not name the spouse, so this is one-in-four on a page and not a marriage. But the certificate behind it would name BOTH FATHERS: William Wear\u2019s father, who is the brick wall at the top of this line \u2014 born about 1823 at Newington, before civil registration \u2014 and Sarah Bloom\u2019s father, which is the whole point of the Yarmouth enquiry. Two of the oldest open questions in this archive now hang on one order.', 'inf'],
  ['A year filter that answers zero instead of filtering', 'The row above had been recorded as a failure: \u201cnot found, and the failure is recorded\u201d. It was not the family, it was the search box. On Findmypast\u2019s England & Wales Marriages, PUTTING A YEAR ON A SEARCH SILENTLY RETURNS NOTHING. The control that proves it: James Shardalow Mort married at Yarmouth in 1885, the row was read on screen \u2014 then searched again for that same surname and place with the year set to 1885 give or take two, and it returned NOTHING AT ALL. Both parameter spellings do it. The casualty here was measured: this archive had recorded three Sarah Bloom marriages in 1841\u20131853, and unfiltered the search returns SIXTY-SEVEN. Every year-filtered marriage search run here has to be redone.', 'disp'],
  ['Counted four sections of a book and called it one family', 'The Berlin address-book sweep reported LEIBHOLZ page hits falling from 24 in 1933 to 2 in 1943 and read it as a surname leaving a city. A Berlin volume has FOUR SECTIONS: householders and firms, the trades index, institutions, and the street directory. In 1933, twenty-four pages carried the name AND EXACTLY ONE OF THEM WAS THE HOUSEHOLDER SECTION. The other twenty-three were the street and trade indexes, where a single household generates a dozen hits. The count measured the size of the book as much as the size of the family, and the Thimm control did not catch it because the control had the same flaw. The name section has now been read on its own, volume by volume, 1920 to 1943.', 'disp'],
  ['Read a garment presser off a page and told two Alfreds apart', 'The 1933 householder section, page 1671: \u201cLuwinSki Alfred B\u00fcgler N31 Putbusser Str 24\u201d \u2014 ALFRED LUWINSKI, A GARMENT PRESSER, A HEAD OF HOUSEHOLD in Wedding. The Arolsen index holds an Alfred Luwinski born 09.09.1917, and a boy of sixteen is neither a householder nor a qualified presser. THEY ARE NOT THE SAME MAN. Father and son is the likeliest reading and the archive does not assert even that. Luwinski reaches the householder section in ONE YEAR of the entire run, which is how rare the name is in Berlin.', 'doc'],
  ['The family was in the rag trade on both sides of the equator', 'The Leibholz entries in the Berlin householder section are not scattered families. They are BUSINESSES, and they are all clothing: Jaques Leibholz & Co., KONFEKTION, W8 Kronenstra\u00dfe; M. Leibholz, TRIKOTAGEN und WOLLWAREN, C2 Rosenstra\u00dfe 17; and SAMUEL LEIBHOLZ, \u201cH\u00dcTE EN GROS\u201d \u2014 HATS, WHOLESALE. Kurt Luwinski kept a hat shop, the Piccadilly Hat, at 81 Eloff Street, Johannesburg. Lizzy was a dressmaker and milliner. The trade did not begin in Johannesburg. Whether the hat wholesaler IS Samuel Israel Leibholz of the Auschwitz death register is a candidate and not a finding \u2014 same uncommon surname, same city, same decade, and two addresses that have not yet been compared.', 'inf'],
  ['Refused to publish a disappearance that the page could not support', 'From the 1939 volume onward the only thing the text layer returns under LEIBHOLZ is the printer\u2019s cross-reference \u201csee also Leipholz\u201d and a LEIBHOLD Wilhelm, who is a different surname. Written as a finding that would have read beautifully: the name leaving the register of Berlin householders in 1939. IT DOES NOT HOLD. Page 1719 of 1939 comes back as ONE UNBROKEN BLOCK of seventeen thousand characters with the columns interleaved, and its running head still says Leibholz. Whether an entry sits beneath it is not legible. Counting works; reading a named line needs the page image, which was written down as a risk before the run and is now a measured fact.', 'disp'],
  ['Martha Giddings did not die in childbirth', 'Three General Register Office entries, all Hitchin: the marriage of George Castle to a Giddings in Q3 1862; the birth of GEORGE WILLIAM CASTLE in Q4 1862, within three months of the wedding; and the death of MARTHA CASTLE in Q3 1863, volume 3A page 188. She died nine to twelve months after her son was born, not at his birth. That also corrects a sentence in an unsent letter \u2014 \u201cshe cannot have known her son at all\u201d \u2014 which is wrong by about nine months. The bound is stated rather than buried: the marriage index does not print the bride\u2019s forename and the pre-1866 death index carries no age, so two certificates are still needed to close it.', 'doc'],
  ['Lost a woman to the surname Smith, and measured how completely', 'Ada May Castle married Sidney Smith, and that is where she leaves the record. The England and Wales death index gives THREE HUNDRED AND SIXTY-ONE Ada May Smiths born 1885\u20131889. Narrowed to Hertfordshire with a wildcard: seventeen Ada Smiths and not one carries the middle name MAY. The 1939 Register, which prints exact dates of birth and would have keyed the death index in a single step, holds two Ada Smiths in the whole county and neither is she \u2014 and her husband is not at Hitchin in 1939 either. They left the county. A national search on that surname and that forename is not a search.', 'inf'],
  ['Counted an index instead of trusting its title, twice in one morning', 'FamilySearch names a collection \u201cSouth Africa, Civil Marriage Records, 1801-1974\u201d. Counted year by year it holds 178,037 marriages for 1960 and ONE HUNDRED AND TWENTY-FOUR for 1961. It is a name index to 1960. The death collection called 1953-1967 is a name index to 1966: 59,263 entries for that year, 178 for the next. THIS MATTERS BECAUSE OF WHO FALLS ON THE FAR SIDE OF IT \u2014 Kurt died in 1968, Henry Robert in September 1969, Leonard in 1973 or 1979. All three are missing from an index that stops before they died, and a fortnight of searching had been reading that absence as a fact about them. It is a fact about the index.', 'disp'],
  ['Read every South African record FamilySearch holds for this family', 'The work list said eleven record sets. All 3,502 FamilySearch collections were enumerated and filtered: THERE ARE THIRTY-TWO. Matching the surname exactly rather than loosely drops 16,156 rows to 111, and all 111 were read. TWO NEW RECORDS. PHYLLIS AGNES WEAR MARRIED ROBERT SELWYN NORQUOY ON 14 JUNE 1952 at Malvern \u2014 which is where the defendant\u2019s surname in WLD 790/1957 comes from, a name this archive had from a court index and could not explain. And KATHLEEN EDITH WEAR AND JAMES CECIL FOOT, 26 May 1945, in an ANGLICAN parish register: the marriage was known from a transcription, the record was not, and it is the first documentary evidence of this family\u2019s denomination in South Africa.', 'doc'],
  ['Wrote up three discoveries that were already on this site', 'The same sweep produced what looked like five new records. Three of them are published here already, and one of them far better than an index can put it \u2014 Ivone\u2019s first marriage at GERMISTON on 2 February 1948 has been READ, entry 27 before magistrate A. J. Rossouw, and Frances\u2019s remarriage to Rolf Eisenhardt on 23 May 1958 has been on Kurt\u2019s page for days. The error cost nothing because the site was checked before the page was written, but it is recorded rather than quietly fixed: AN INDEX AGREEING WITH A DOCUMENT YOU HAVE ALREADY READ IS CORROBORATION, NOT A FINDING, and a tool that answers in two seconds makes it very easy to forget which of the two you are looking at.', 'disp'],
  ['Kurt\u2019s third marriage was not the end of his story', 'FRANCES HOSTOWSKY, BERNER, SEFTON, LUWINSKI \u2014 the surname stack is four marriages \u2014 REMARRIED ON 23 MAY 1958, to Rolf Eisenhardt. She had married Kurt on 29 February 1956. That marriage lasted two years and three months, and Kurt lived another TEN YEARS after it ended. The archive has been closing his life at the 1956 register entry. There is a divorce between those two dates, in the same court as the five Luwinski files already held, and nobody has looked for it.', 'inf'],
  ['Found the one document that would answer everything, and it is not online yet', 'A South African DEATH NOTICE names the dead man\u2019s parents. FamilySearch holds \u201cSouth Africa, Transvaal, Estate Files, 1950-1993\u201d \u2014 191,580 images of exactly those notices, from the National Archives in Pretoria \u2014 and its title brackets Kurt in 1968, Henry Robert in 1969, both of Leonard\u2019s candidate years and Martha Maud in 1986. ITS WAYPOINTS SAY IT IS PUBLISHED FOR 1955 TO 1960 ONLY. Seven of them. The Orange Free State equivalent has fifty-seven, running to 2006, so the gap is a publishing schedule and not a destroyed record. Checking whether that has changed costs one request and returns a number.', 'inf'],
  ['Leaked three living match holders into a build, and found out from a guard that was not running', 'Two named living people reached the published pages and a third reached the work list. The check that exists precisely to stop this — it compares every published page against the private match list — HAD NEVER BEEN WIRED INTO THE BUILD CHAIN. It is now, and it runs on every build: 111 match holders checked against every page. All three names were stripped. A guard that is written and not called is not a guard.', 'disp'],
  ['CORRECTED: Martha Giddings had her son for something under a year', 'This archive wrote, and put in a letter to a living second cousin, that if Martha Giddings died in 1863 she could not have known her son at all. SHE DIED IN THE THIRD QUARTER of 1863; GEORGE WILLIAM WAS BAPTISED ON 4 JANUARY of it. Two numbers, both already on file, and the quarters were never compared. It changes the reading of the 1871 census as well: the widower is IN the Giddins house at Norton End, enumerated as son-in-law with his boy as grandson, so he did not hand the child over — he went to his dead wife\'s parents and stayed eight years. A correction has been drafted to the cousin who was told the wrong thing.', 'disp'],
  ['Found Caroline where nobody could follow her, because she changed her name', 'Every tree loses Caroline Wallis after her husband died in 1916. She remarried in Q3 1920 at Hitchin — CAROLINE CASTLE to JAMES JAMES — and was CAROLINE JAMES for the last thirty-seven years of her life, which is why her death in 1957 could not be found under either of the surnames people were searching. The 1939 Register has her at 38 Norton Road, Baldock, widowed a second time, and gives her exact date of birth: 28 FEBRUARY 1863, not the 1862 every secondary source repeats.', 'doc'],
  ['The transport has a number, and the memorial book was not the one we thought', 'Yad Vashem had been used here for exactly one list. Searched properly, LEIBHOLZ returns 88 victim records across four collections — and Jacob\'s record carries a field this archive never had: "Details of Transport: TRANSPORT 30 from Berlin to Auschwitz Birkenau, Extermination Camp, Poland on 26/02/1943." The date has been on these pages for a fortnight. THE TRAIN HAD NOT. It also gives his citizenship as GERMANY, which matters to the statelessness argument two pages away. OSKAR\'S RECORD closes the distance between his two deportations: Berlin to Theresienstadt 17 March 1943, Theresienstadt to Auschwitz 16 October 1944, and DATE OF DEATH 16 OCTOBER 1944 — the same day the second transport left. AND THE SOURCE BEHIND THE TRANSPORT NUMBER IS A BOOK THIS ARCHIVE DID NOT KNOW IT WAS STANDING ON: not the Bundesarchiv Gedenkbuch cited on every page here, but the GEDENKBUCH BERLINS DER JÜDISCHEN OPFER DES NATIONALSOZIALISMUS, Freie Universität Berlin, Edition Hentrich, 1995 — Berlin\'s own. It has never been consulted directly and it should be. One discrepancy settles against Arolsen and in the Bundesarchiv\'s favour: Yad Vashem has GEORG born 1884, not 1885. And one decoy is written down before it costs anybody a day — "KURT LEIBHOLZ, 1927, Berlin, murdered" IS NOT OUR KURT, who was a Luwinski, born 1912, and died in Johannesburg in 1968.', 'doc'],
  ['Three of them are on one document, and one of the three had been gone ten years', 'The Arolsen Archives had sat on the sources page under "identified, not yet worked" since 14 September. Worked on the 17th. The index needs no consent from anybody and it holds 213 Leibholz entries. It confirms JAKOB 08.08.1884 SCHUBIN and OSKAR 13.05.1887 SCHUBIN from records with no connection to the Bundesarchiv — and only those two Leibholzes in the whole index are recorded as born at Schubin. It carries two of the brothers under the compulsory name, LEIBHOLZ ISRAEL OSKAR and LEIBHOLZ GEORG ISRAEL, which puts the 1938 decree published here this morning onto the family\'s own paper. It holds a post-war tracing case for Oskar, T/D 52.971, fourteen documents, which exists because somebody asked. AND THEN A SEARCH FOR AUGUSTE RETURNED SOMEBODY THIS ARCHIVE DID NOT KNOW WAS IN THE PERSECUTION RECORDS AT ALL: "LEIBHOLZ, Auguste, maiden name LUWWINSKY, born 28.08.1893". That is Lizzy, carried here for a fortnight as 1893-1975 with no date — and the maiden name is spelt with a DOUBLED W, a seventh form of the surname and the first that is German rather than South African. THEN THE DOCUMENT IDENTIFIERS WERE COMPARED. Jacob, Auguste and Kurt ALL RESOLVE TO DocID 11214358, folder MFN01335, under Deportations and Transports. IT IS ONE DOCUMENT. The father who was deported, the mother who was not, and the son who had been in Johannesburg for ten years, all three named on a single sheet. This morning it was written here that Kurt was probably "named inside a document about somebody else"; by the afternoon the somebody else had a name and it was his father. WHAT STAYS LABELLED: the shared DocID is a hard fact from the index; what kind of document it is, is not, because nobody here has read it — opening one requires ticking a declaration of consent about the use of sensitive personal data, and this archive does not tick that on anybody\'s behalf.', 'doc'],
  ['A surname leaving a city, and a control that proves it is not the scanner', 'David asked what more could be done on the Leibholz and Luwinski side. THE WHOLE LINE LIVED IN BERLIN AND THIS ARCHIVE HAD NEVER LOOKED IN THE BERLIN DIRECTORY. The Zentral- und Landesbibliothek has digitised the Berliner Adressbücher from 1799 to 1970 — one volume a year, six to seven thousand pages each, householders by NAME, TRADE AND STREET — free, no account, answering a JSON query. 1,047 page hits for LEIBHOLZ, 23 for LUWINSKI. THE FIRST THING OUT OF IT: Leibholz page hits per annual volume run 24 in 1933 and hold between 15 and 24 through the thirties — then 12 in 1940, FIVE in 1941, six in 1942, TWO IN 1943. AND THE CONTROL IS NOT AN IDLE ONE. THIMM — Johanna Thimm of Sauerbaum is Kurt\'s own great-grandmother, East Prussian and not Jewish — runs 66, 53, 47, 65, 72, 68, 45, 75, 69, 63 AND 75 IN 1943: as common in the last volume as in the first. The scanner did not get worse, the Fraktur did not get harder, the book did not get smaller. THE LIMITS GO IN WITH IT AND THEY MATTER MORE THAN THE NUMBER: a page hit is a page and not a person, twenty-four to two is a small base, and DEPORTATION, EMIGRATION AND ADMINISTRATIVE EXCLUSION FROM DIRECTORIES ALL LOOK IDENTICAL IN A COUNT — Jews were being struck out of German commercial listings from 1938 quite apart from being deported, and a count cannot tell those apart. AND A LEAD: the Luwinskis of Berlin are in the garment trade. ALFRED LUWINSKI, BÜGLER — a presser — at Putbusser Straße 24 in Wedding in 1933 and 1936, a lorry driver by 1943, while Lizzy was a dressmaker and milliner in the same city. A brother is the obvious guess and it is a guess.', 'doc'],
  ['Two hundred and nineteen commits citing Kew, and the catalogue had never been opened', 'David asked whether this archive had ever looked at The National Archives. IT HAD NOT. The pages carry RG10, RG11, RG12, RG13, RG14, RG15, BT 27, WO 329, WO 372 and WO 392 — hundreds of references to records held at Kew — and every one came second-hand through a commercial index\'s transcript. Discovery is free, needs no account and answers JSON. The first hour produced four things. EVERY CITATION VERIFIES, and two verify BETTER than they were published: WO 392/15 is specifically "Section 5: South Africa (Union Defence Forces)", which is exactly the claim this archive makes about Leonard Wear, and WO 329/1120 adds the page range 312-622. WO 372/4/50790 IS THE EXACT ITEM REFERENCE for Fred James Castle\'s medal card, in Kew\'s own words — "Scottish Rifles 10061 Serjeant. Scottish Rifles 7815432 Serjeant. Machine Gun Corps 195568 Serjeant." Findmypast gave only the piece and PRINTED THE DISCOVERY ID ON ITS OWN TRANSCRIPT; this archive had it for hours and never followed it. DP/12/1/14 AND DP/12/1/15 ARE THE BALDOCK BAPTISM REGISTERS, 1880-1895 and 1895-1910, at Hertfordshire Archives — the register this archive had been gesturing at all day, now an orderable reference. And LUWINSKI RETURNS ZERO across 2,500+ British archives, filed with the reason a catalogue is not a name index. A reusable tool went in with it, tools/tna.py, because the failure here was not ignorance of the source but never having reached for it.', 'doc'],
  ['Found him: Sergeant 10061, Cameronians (Scottish Rifles), and a career soldier into the 1920s', 'FREDERICK JAMES CASTLE is the only Castle male line this archive has and he had been missing from every English record since 1901. He is found, and the chain runs: the 1901 census has him at eleven at NORTON END, BALDOCK, son of George W and Caroline; the 1911 census has a Frederick Castle, 21, single, born Herts Baldock, in MEEANEE BARRACKS, COLCHESTER — and the ORIGINAL IMAGE, read at 299 per cent, gives column 5, UNIT OR ARM OF SERVICE, as SCOTTISH RIFLES, which the transcript leaves blank; and the campaign medal roll, TNA WO 329/1120, gives FRED JAMES CASTLE, SERGEANT, 10061, CAMERONIANS (SCOTTISH RIFLES), British War Medal and Victory Medal. THIS MORNING THE IDENTIFICATION RESTED ON ONE CENSUS AGE. It now rests on two matches arrived at from opposite ends — the forenames FRED JAMES, exactly as the General Register Office registered him in 1889, and the regiment, exactly as an enumerator wrote it over his line in 1911. AND HIS MEDAL INDEX CARD, WO 372/4, EXPLAINS EVERY ABSENCE THE ROW WAS BUILT ON: Sergeant throughout, THREE service numbers — 10061 Scottish Rifles, 195568 MACHINE GUN CORPS, and 7815432 Scottish Rifles. An eight-digit number is a post-1920 issue. HE WAS STILL A SERGEANT AFTER THE WAR, which is why he is in no 1921 household, no 1939 Register and no English death index. Not proved, and the file says so: the medal roll carries no birth date and no birthplace, so the link is forenames plus regiment and not a document stating identity. A service record would state it, because it names next of kin.', 'inf'],
  ['Nine born, eight living, one died — and all nine are now named', 'The 1911 census asks a married woman how many children she has borne. Caroline answered NINE. This archive could name seven, and the baptism register turned out to stop in 1879. It closed anyway, from the household. THE 1901 CENSUS HAS THE FAMILY WHOLE — RG13/1298 folio 49 page 26, schedule 192, NORTON END, NORTON, BALDOCK: George W Castle 36, Caroline 36 born WESTON, and five children including FREDERICK, 11, and MARTHA, 5. THEY HAD GONE BACK TO NORTON END, the hamlet where George William was himself raised by his GIDDINS grandparents after his mother died; he brought his own family home to it. THE NINTH CHILD IS SARAH ANN WALLIS, born 19 August 1882 at Weston, TWO MONTHS BEFORE HER PARENTS MARRIED AND REGISTERED UNDER HER MOTHER\'S MAIDEN NAME — not a Castle birth in the index at all, which is why no search for Castle births could have found her. AND THE ONE WHO DIED IS NAMED: SIDNEY CHARLES CASTLE, born Q2 1898 at Hitchin, DIED Q4 1900 AT HITCHIN, AGED TWO. One entry in each index, no competitors, and he was gone five months before the 1901 census, which is why no census ever showed him. The arithmetic closes exactly. AND THE SIX-INSTEAD-OF-NINE WAS THREE SEPARATE INDEXING FAILURES: Sarah Ann filed under Wallis, Martha Maud with a blank mother\'s maiden name, Sidney Charles missing too. A filter on a field that is sometimes empty is a filter on the indexing, not on the family.', 'doc'],
  ['Frederick Castle was a soldier, which is why nobody could find him at home', 'He is in the 1901 census after all, aged 11 at Norton End — the earlier search that came back wrong had found the OTHER Norton End Castle family, three schedules away on the same night. And in 1911 there is a BALDOCK-BORN FREDERICK CASTLE, 21, SINGLE, AT MEEANEE BARRACKS, COLCHESTER: RG14 piece 10304, schedule 9999, Military Road and Mersea Road. A regular soldier is not in anybody\'s household. The age is the discriminator and it is thin but real — 21 on 2 April 1911 means born between April 1889 and April 1890, which fits ours and excludes both of the other Baldock Freds. IT IS NOT PROOF and it is labelled probable: the entry says Frederick, not Frederick James, and it rests on one age and a birthplace. What it changes is the shape of the question. The 1921 census holds ONE Baldock-born Frederick Castle in all England and Wales and it is the other family\'s; ours is out of the country by 1921, is not in Soldiers Died, and is in no death index. A regular soldier who vanishes like that went abroad and stayed, or died in a service Soldiers Died does not cover.', 'inf'],
  ['Part of this morning\'s withdrawal comes back, in a different record set', 'The electoral registers were withdrawn today because the set tested — 1832 to 1932 — returns page images and indexes no elector at all. There is a SECOND AND DIFFERENT SET, ENGLAND & WALES ELECTORAL REGISTERS 1910-1932, AND IT CARRIES NAMES: a search for Castle in Hertfordshire returns Arthur Frederick Castle in 1929 and 1930, Frederick Castle in 1919 and 1920. It cannot do what the withdrawn row wanted, because it starts in 1910 and the question was Bermondsey 1885 to 1900. It can place the Baldock Castles year by year from 1919 — the decade in which Caroline became Caroline James and Frederick disappeared. The withdrawal stands; what was wrong was the claim about one set, not the idea.', 'doc'],
  ['Koszalin answered, and the record that would settle Georg Leibholz does not exist', 'The first of eleven institutional letters to come back. Reference O-S.6342.406.2026, signed by the Head of the Slupsk Branch: the archive holds NO registers of the German civil registry office at CZARNE — Standesamt HAMMERSTEIN, Kreis Schlochau — for 1874 to 1899, and NO books of the Jewish community there at all, and NO information about what became of either or where they might now be. That last clause is what makes it a good letter rather than a brush-off. Hammerstein is where GEORG LEIBHOLZ was born on 7 June 1884, and an 1884 birth entry is the one document that would name his parents and settle whether he belongs to this family. This archive holds that he does not — Jacob was born at SCHUBIN in Posen on 8 August 1884, two months later and in another province — and that finding now rests on the Gedenkbuch alone, because the record that could overturn it is gone. SALLY LEIBHOLZ, born 1847, predates civil registration entirely, so only the synagogue books could have held him, and those are the ones that do not exist. Acknowledged the same day, in Polish, in their own thread.', 'doc'],
  ['The baptism register ends five years before the family starts', 'The 1911 census has Caroline answering NINE CHILDREN BORN, EIGHT LIVING, ONE DIED, and this archive can name seven. The baptism register names both parents on every line, so it should have named all nine. ALL 823 CASTLE BAPTISMS IN THE HERTFORDSHIRE SET WERE SORTED BY MOTHER\'S FIRST NAME AND THE CAROLINE BLOCK READ: between the Barbara block and the Catharine block there is EXACTLY ONE ROW. The reason is a coverage bound and not a search failure — sorted by year, the latest of 195 Castle baptisms at Baldock is 1879, and the children were born 1884 to 1904. Martha Maud\'s own Baldock baptism of September 1895 is not in the set either. THE INSTRUMENT ENDS FIVE YEARS BEFORE THE FAMILY STARTS, the two unnamed children stay unnamed, and the answer is in the parish register at Hertfordshire Archives rather than in anything searchable from here. THE ONE ROW IS WORTH HAVING: ADA MAY CASTLE, BAPTISED 10 APRIL 1887 AT WESTON, father GEORGE WILLIAM CASTLE, LABOURER, mother CAROLINE. The first baptism read for any of Caroline\'s children, and it puts the family at Weston — her own birthplace, and where Sarah Ann Wallis was born two months before the marriage.', 'doc'],
  ['Ada May did not die a Castle, and a second woman goes missing the same way', 'The harvested match-holder\'s tree records "Ada May Castle, died Jul 1969, Hitchin". SHE MARRIED IN Q4 1909 AT HITCHIN, volume 3A page 1171, aged twenty-two. And the death index has no such entry: Ada-anything Castle born 1884 to 1890, all England and Wales, returns sixteen rows, NOT ONE at Hitchin and NOT ONE in 1969. A woman who married in 1909 did not die a Castle sixty years later. This is the CAROLINE JAMES trap in miniature, and it is the second time in two days the same mechanism has hidden a woman from this archive — a tree recording a death under a maiden name its owner had long stopped using. Her married surname is NOT guessed here: MarriageFinder offers two page-mates from the index and page-mates are not a marriage. The general lesson is worth more than Ada May — every married daughter in that tree with a death recorded under CASTLE is now suspect.', 'disp'],
  ['Called a result count a source, for the third time in a week', 'Yesterday this archive filed a row saying the England and Wales Electoral Registers had returned NINE ENTRIES FOR WILLIAM WEAR, Surrey and London, 1885 to 1900, and called them the best unworked source for the Bermondsey Wears — an ANNUAL address record covering the years that bracket both brothers\' deaths. TODAY ONE WAS OPENED. Every row in that record set says "NO NAME ON RECORD" and carries a PDF icon: they are page images of volumes, not entries about people. Searched without a place filter, WILLIAM WEAR returns 3,478 of them in DEVON and DURHAM — Weare in Devon, the River Wear in Durham. JOHN SMITH returns the same nameless rows in Lancashire. LUWINSKI returns zero, and so does a nonsense string, so the fields are not inert; they simply match a place. The "Surrey and London" came from a place filter typed by the same hand that then reported it as a finding. A RESULT COUNT IS A PROPERTY OF A SEARCH BOX. This archive has now made that mistake over two Giddins DNA matches, over two archives-index zeroes that were three records each, and here — and it wrote the warning after the first one. WHAT SURVIVES: the Bermondsey volumes are digitised and can be READ page by page for 1885 to 1900. It is an afternoon of images, not a search, and the row now says so.', 'disp'],
  ['TWENTY-ONE EVIDENCE FILES AND NOT ONE BUILD SCRIPT READ ANY OF THEM', 'Asked to check for a fault the sister archive at Mazza had just been caught by — a per-person builder that never opened its own evidence files, so twelve documented people were told on their own pages that nothing had been read about them. This archive has no per-person pages, so it could not fail in that exact shape. IT FAILED IN A WORSE ONE. Every file in data/ was audited against every build script: build-register.py reads the MyHeritage export and corrections.json, the two living-persons guards read the export and a hash file, and THAT IS THE WHOLE LIST. All twenty-one .tsv evidence files — the Gedenkbuch, six censuses, the 1939 Register, the passenger lists, the court files, the Bermondsey households — were read by NOTHING. Their contents reached the site only where a human had retyped them into a paragraph. The clearest casualty: the register printed Samuel Israel Leibholz\'s birth as "June 28 1877?", the source tree\'s question mark intact, while data/leibholz-gedenkbuch.tsv held Bundesarchiv entry en909753 giving 28 June 1877 at Schubin and his death at Auschwitz on 10 April 1943. The archive had the citation and the page showed the doubt. Eleven people — three Blooms of Yarmouth, four Hawkinses of Watford, two Castles, Gertrude Wear, a Letchworth lodger — were documented in the repository and appeared NOWHERE on the published site at all.', 'disp'],
  ['Built the evidence in, and then a gate so it cannot fall out again', 'tools/build-evidence.py now reads every evidence file through an EXPLICIT MANIFEST and writes what each record says about each person; /evidence/ publishes all 135 records about 100 people; and the register carries a RECORD READ column. tools/check-evidence.py runs on every build and refuses it three ways: a file in data/ the manifest does not declare, a stale build, or a person named in an evidence file reaching the published site with neither a record nor a correction. All four failure modes were reintroduced deliberately and all four exit 1. AND IT CHANGES NO DATE. The register and the charts still draw the export as given — the question mark on Samuel\'s birth is still there, because that is what the source says. The record now sits beside it instead of in a file nobody reads. Where a named source genuinely overrides the family record that is a correction, carried as was and now, and it is a different mechanism on purpose.', 'doc'],
  ['The new builder hung three divorce actions on the wrong Kurt, and the fix is that it now refuses to choose', 'Written in the same hour as the entry above, and caught by reading its own output. The register holds KURT LUWINSKI, died 1995, a surname-index entry, and KURT ISRAEL LUWINSKI, 1912 to 1968, who is the man this archive is about. The linker tested exact names first and only fell back to first-plus-last, so the index man matched exactly and won while the real one never got a vote — and WLD 1950/1948, WLD 497/1951 and WLD 2368/1951 were attached to a stranger who outlived Kurt by twenty-seven years. It now pools every candidate before deciding and LINKS NEITHER when more than one person answers to a name, naming both on the page so a human resolves it. A name match is not a person match. This archive has now said that about a census index, a surname-tagged DNA match, three Frederick Castles, eighteen Bermondsey Wears and two Emma Hawkinses — and then written a script that did it anyway. The same check also turned up a DUPLICATE the register\'s own collapse list does not cover: Johannes Antonie Oelofse De Witt is in the source twice, once with a death date and once without.', 'disp'],
  ['And the second guard caught the first one leaking', 'The first build of the new evidence page failed check-matches: a free-text note in the match-derived Castle file identifies a living DNA match holder as somebody\'s grandson, and publishing the file published him. The builder now carries a per-file redaction list and that column never reaches the page. The guard written eight days ago, after three living people were leaked into a build, caught the tool written to fix a different problem on its first run.', 'doc'],
  ['Followed the Pix Road household to the end of all four lives', 'The Pix Road find opened one question worth the day: Louisa Shenton was Henry Robert Wear\'s sister, so a descendant of hers would carry the WEAR half of Derrick\'s ancestry and not the Castle half — the independent test this archive has wanted since it read the segments. THE ANSWER IS NO. Births, surname SHENTON, mother\'s maiden name WEAR: one result, STANLEY W SHENTON, Q1 1924, Hitchin. Deaths, surname Shenton, born 1924: THE SAME CHILD, Q1 1924, Hitchin. Born and died in the same quarter, to a mother of forty-one. And the trap that had caught this archive over Martha Maud was checked for: the district was searched with NO mother\'s name at all, Shenton at Hitchin across twenty-one years, and there is exactly one Shenton birth in it. WILLIAM SHENTON married a Wear at Hitchin in Q3 1918 and died there in Q3 1953. LOUISE S A SHENTON died at Hitchin in Q4 1967, aged 84 — and the initials close it, because her birth is registered as LOUISA SARAH A WEAR, Q1 1883, at KINGSTON, SURREY, which is a place neither of her parents had ever been put before. AND EMMA HULL DIED IN Q2 1944 AT HITCHIN, AGED EIGHTY-FIVE, four years after the grandson she had known as a boy was taken prisoner. Eighty-five years from Aldenham to Letchworth, two husbands buried, and thirty miles.', 'doc'],
  ['Wrote a grandmother over her own mother, by matching on a name', 'The Pix Road finding was applied to the tree by a script that selected people BY NAME. Emma Hawkins the daughter, born 7 February 1859, and Emma Hawkins the mother, born about 1822, are two entries two generations apart with identical names — and the code did not look at the generation. For part of 17 September this archive published that a woman born about 1822 was blind at Letchworth in 1939. The same run appended a sentence to the Giddins household at Norton End saying that THEIR daughter Emma was born on 7 February 1859; their Emma is EMMA GIDDINS, born about 1855 at Norton, a different woman in a different parish who shares only a forename. Both are withdrawn on the pages themselves. A match on a name is not a match on a person — which is the thing this archive says about every census index it touches, said while doing it.', 'disp'],
  ['Caroline James stops being an index line and becomes a household', 'The remarriage had rested on a GRO index entry. The 1921 census settles it from both directions. There is NO Caroline Castle anywhere in Hertfordshire in 1921 — she leaves the surname exactly when the index says she changed it. And there is one Caroline James: RG15/7079, schedule 125, 9 BOSCOMBE PLACE, LETCHWORTH, wife of JAMES JAMES, born at WESTON, Hertfordshire — with ERNEST ALBERT CYRIL CASTLE and FLORENCE MARGARITE HILDA CASTLE in the house, enumerated as his STEP-CHILDREN. That is an enumerator writing down a remarriage. Her age, 56 years 4 months, puts her birthday in February and agrees with the 28th; the year is two out and she is the one who gave it. She had also moved to Letchworth, where her daughter was raising the Wears.', 'doc'],
  ['Nine children born, eight living, and only six have been found', 'The 1911 census at St Mary\'s Cottages, Baldock — RG14/7572, schedule 173 — asks a married woman how many children she has borne. Caroline answers NINE BORN, EIGHT LIVING, ONE DIED. This archive has names for six from the GRO index and a seventh in Martha Maud. So at least two Castle children have never been identified and one of the nine was already dead by 1911. Her own father, JOSEPH WALLIS, is in the house as well, spelt Wallis — a seventh independent instance of the spelling that was in dispute a day ago.', 'doc'],
  ['Excluded the wrong Frederick, with an exact date this time', 'Frederick James Castle, born 1889 at Hitchin to a mother née Wallis, is the only Castle male line that could ever carry a Y test. The Frederick James Castle who died at Hitchin in 1975 is NOT him: the 1939 Register has that man at 17 ORCHARD ROAD, BALDOCK, born 10 APRIL 1887, a vertical driller on aircraft work, married to Ella. And Baldock supplies the warning as well as the problem — a search of the 1921 census returns a SECOND unrelated Castle household at 5 Cemetery Road with nine children and its own Frederick, born about April 1888. Three men of one name in one small town within two years. Our Frederick is in no English census after 1901 and in no death index at all, which is now the open question rather than a solved one.', 'inf'],
  ['Sixty-three and seventy-two Pix Road', 'The 1939 Register was searched for Leonard\'s aunt Louisa Shenton and returned his grandmother. Schedule 305, 63 PIX ROAD, LETCHWORTH: EMMA HULL, born 7 February 1859, widowed, "Incapacitated - Blind"; Louisa Shenton, born 29 November 1882; William Shenton, incapacitated. Emma Hull is EMMA HAWKINS — born at Aldenham, widowed at thirty-one in 1890, remarried to John Hull and widowed again. Henry Robert and Martha Maud had raised their six at 72 PIX ROAD, the same street. The register was taken on 29 September 1939, seven weeks after the last ship sailed, and it finds an eighty-year-old blind woman and the daughter keeping her still in the road the family had left. The record says who was where and nothing about what anybody felt. But it is no longer true that the family simply left.', 'doc'],
  ['Learned that the paywall was not one', 'The estate references looked to be behind a subscription. They are not: every South African estate over the threshold must be advertised as a Form J193 notice in the Government Gazette, which is free and full-text to 2026. The only trick is the name order — modern notices read surname first. Four estates in twenty minutes, all four women born Wear, with exact birth dates, identity numbers and addresses.', 'doc'],
  ['Oskar left Theresienstadt on a card that names the transport at both ends', 'His four Arolsen person records were on the work list as \u201call openable and none read\u201d. Read, three of them lock together. The Gestapo list of the \u201c4th Large Transport of the Elderly\u201d, 17 March 1943, page 161 line 54: Leibholz, Oskar Israel, 13.5.87, Schubin, W.50 Marburgerstr. 13, number 014577. An AJDC index card repeats it as \u201c49/30999 II \u2014 4.gr.Alterstrsp. 17.3.43\u201d and adds his birthplace in full, SCHUBIN/BROMBERG, and his wife, GERTRUD geb. LEIBKE, 13.7.94 Berlin. And in Terez\u00edn a Czech clerk wrote out a card of the central transport file: DEPORTACE NA V\u00ddCHOD, \u010c\u00edslo Er 606, dne 16.X.1944, with the original transport number 11025-I/90 \u2014 which is the Berlin transport\u2019s own designation. The two ends of the journey name each other. The card does not say where he was sent; it says east.', 'doc'],
  ['WITHDRAWN: the document that was not a deportation form', 'On 17 September this archive found Jacob, Auguste and Kurt sharing one Arolsen DocID in a series called Deportations and Transports, reasoned that it was \u201ca deportation form that records a man\u2019s household and his next of kin\u201d, and called that reading \u201cevidence instead of the charitable guess\u201d. David ticked the consent and the document was opened. IT IS A BERLIN RESIDENTS\u2019 REGISTRATION CARD, and the DocID covers two separate images \u2014 the family\u2019s card and a sheet of other households. KURT\u2019S NAME IS ON THE SECOND SHEET, not on his family\u2019s card. The series name was read as the document. What the card does give is worth the correction: AUGUSTE\u2019S BIRTHPLACE IN HER OWN HOUSEHOLD\u2019S HAND \u2014 SAUERBAUM, the East Prussian village her mother Johanna Thimm came from, which until today rested on a single census card.', 'disp'],
  ['Tested a restriction instead of assuming it', 'T/D 755.334 for Heinz Leibholz was listed as \u201cmay be restricted like Oskar\u2019s \u2014 BUT THAT HAS NOT BEEN TESTED\u201d. Tested: eleven documents, creation date 1959\u20131988, and the page serves no DocID, no image and no document list. The consent declaration does not reach the T/D repository at all. The same sweep listed every Leibholz topic in the archive \u2014 forty-three of them \u2014 and the list is itself a finding: ten consecutive case numbers, 90.465 to 90.474, are one enquiry about one household, and they match the Levetzowstra\u00dfe 13 family from the 1939 census to the day. One of those ten is \u201cLEIBHOLZ, KURT\u201d, and it is Alfred\u2019s son born in 1927, not Derrick\u2019s father. Written down so nobody finds it later and draws the obvious wrong conclusion.', 'doc'],
  ['An emigration card with the word emigrating crossed out', 'Row 197 was written to ask whether Oskar\u2019s wife was on the transport that took him to Theresienstadt, because she is not on the page that carries him. It was answered the same afternoon and the answer is no. The Kultusvereinigung Berlin \u2014 the community organisation the state had turned into an instrument of its own administration \u2014 kept Z\u00e4hlkarten, counting cards, and hers survives: \u201cLeibholz geb. Leibke \u00b7 Gertrud \u00b7 13.7.94, Berlin \u00b7 verh.\u201d, household address Marburgerstr. 13, written on 8 March 1943. The card is headed \u201cZ\u00e4hlkarte f\u00fcr AUSWANDERNDE Personen\u201d with AUSWANDERNDE struck through in ink, and in the column headed ZIELLAND \u2014 country of destination \u2014 somebody has written VERSTORBEN 27/2.43. She died eighteen days before the transport left. Oskar\u2019s line on the list carries no Ehepaar bracket because by then there was no pair. The date itself was already published here from the Memorial Book, which is a compilation; it is now on a card filled in nine days later by the office that knew her.', 'doc'],
  ['Two Leibholz families, and they are finally separable by place', 'The 43 tracing topics threw up \u201cT/D 161.094 for MEYER, GERTRUD born 09.03.1899\u201d, indexed under the maiden name LEIBHOLZ. A Leibholz daughter born in 1899 would have fitted the Schubin family, which has no sister in it at all, and a row was written to chase her. Her second person record answers it in one word: PLACE OF BIRTH GLOWITZ \u2014 G\u0142\u00f3wczyce, KREIS STOLP, in Pomerania. That is Willi\u2019s district, not Jacob\u2019s. She is a Pomeranian Leibholz and she is not anybody\u2019s sister here. THE NEGATIVE IS WORTH MORE THAN THE HYPOTHESIS WAS: for weeks the two Leibholz families could only be told apart by argument, and now they can be told apart by place \u2014 SCHUBIN IN POSEN against STOLP IN POMMERN, with two documented birthplaces on each side. Her own fate is on the record: Wave 48, the 34th Osttransport to Auschwitz, 4 March 1943 \u2014 thirteen days before Oskar went to Theresienstadt, on a different kind of list altogether.', 'doc'],
  ['Searched the names database by who WROTE the page, and found somebody alive', 'A Page of Testimony names the person who filled it in, and so does a Relico questionnaire, so searching the SUBMITTER field asks a different question: not who died but WHO SURVIVED TO WRITE IT DOWN. Submitter last name LEIBHOLZ returns three records and one woman \u2014 SOPHIE LEIBHOLZ, filling in forms for the World Jewish Congress relief committee IN SHANGHAI IN 1946. She was looking for her brother SALLY BINDER, a tailor of Motzstrasse 13 in Neuk\u00f6lln, deported to Auschwitz on 1 March 1943; for his wife Mathilde; and for her brother-in-law HEINRICH LEIBHOLZ, born Berlin 16 October 1890, a tax agent of Hornstrasse 19, wife Agnes. Heinrich is this archive\u2019s control case \u2014 the man used to prove that vanishing from the Berlin directory after 1942 does not mean death, because he died in 1948. He now has a birth date, a wife, a street and a trade, and somebody was looking for him from Shanghai two years before he died, not knowing he had lived. Nothing ties any of them to Kurt\u2019s family, and it is the first Leibholz survivor this archive has a name for.', 'doc'],
  ['WITHDRAWN: the niece who was a niece by marriage', 'The work list has argued for three days that IRMGARD SHTERN was the best lead in the archive on the oldest open question \u2014 whether Georg Leibholz\u2019s line touches Sally Leibholz and Friederike Schmul of Schubin. The argument was clean: she called herself Georg\u2019s NIECE, so her parent was Georg\u2019s sibling, so her other Pages of Testimony would name GEORG\u2019S PARENTS. She filed seventeen of them, and three of them end it. Lidia Laibholz b.1884 gives her father as ELIUS and her mother as MINA; Klara Lewinzon b.1906 gives YULIUS and MINA; Ema Mendelzon b.1892 gives YULIUS and MEINE. Elius and Yulius are one name through the Hebrew and so are Mina and Meine. THEY ARE THREE SISTERS, AND IRMGARD IS NIECE TO ALL THREE \u2014 so Georg is the uncle BY MARRIAGE, her blood is Lydia\u2019s family, and her Pages could never have given Georg\u2019s parents. Georg\u2019s own Page has no father and no mother on it and gives his birthplace as the single word Germany. WHAT THE SEVENTEEN DID GIVE: Lydia\u2019s parents and two sisters, which nobody had \u2014 and GIDEON SHTRUZIK, BORN 1940, marital status \u201cchild\u201d, of Berlin, murdered, father Harry. Harry Struzik was Elli Leibholz\u2019s husband, so Gideon is almost certainly Elli\u2019s son and Georg\u2019s grandson, although his mother is not named on the page.', 'disp'],
  ['Followed a survivor to Shanghai and found her husband\u2019s death certificate in a list', 'This morning\u2019s find was a name and a city: SOPHIE LEIBHOLZ, filling in relief questionnaires in Shanghai in 1946, the first Leibholz survivor this archive had a name for. Who she was married to was unknown. Mapping the Lives has an EMIGRATION PLACE field nobody here had used \u2014 surname LEIBHOLZ, emigration place SHANGHAI, two results, and one of them is ALBERT LEIBHOLZ born 17 May 1886, who is the \u201cAlbert, Antiquit\u00e4ten, Halensee\u201d of the 1938 Berlin directory. Arolsen then has him twice. A CENTRAL LOCATION INDEX card, L 337909: \u201cLeibholz \u00b7 Albert \u00b7 died 8.18.44 \u00b7 5.17.86 \u00b7 Berlin \u00b7 dealer \u00b7 last known address SHANGHAI\u201d \u2014 and under OTHER MEMBERS OF THE FAMILY ABROAD, \u201cSOPHIE \u00b7 W \u00b7 6.19.87 \u00b7 Berlin\u201d. W is wife. The card also carries the crossing \u2014 \u201carr. 6.27.39 from Berlin\u201d \u2014 and a third person looking for them, Curt Sterngast of Rehavia, Jerusalem. And the H.I.A.S. list of Jewish nationals who died and were buried in Shanghai 1939\u20131948, page 24: \u201cLEIBHOLZ, ALBERT \u00b7 5/17/86 \u00b7 8/18/44 \u00b7 paralisis progressitis\u201d. A Berlin antiques dealer, out on 27 June 1939 with ten weeks to spare, dead in Shanghai in 1944, his widow still there in December 1945. It also separates the two Sophie Leibholzes for good \u2014 n\u00e9e Binder, born Berlin 19 June 1887, alive; n\u00e9e Rothenberg, born Schlochau 9 January 1887, murdered. None of it touches Kurt\u2019s family, and it is the first Leibholz household this archive has followed all the way out.', 'doc'],
  ['Searched the census by address instead of by name, and found our own Kreis in Charlottenburg', 'Every earlier pass at the 1939 census database started from a surname in the Berlin directory. Starting from a STREET AND HOUSE NUMBER returns the whole flat, and it produced a correction and a household. The correction is this archive\u2019s own: Levetzowstra\u00dfe 13 holds SIX Leibholz, not five \u2014 WALTER, born 20.09.1922, was simply missed, and he is the T/D case 90.473 that looked like a name the census lacked. The household is Danckelmannstra\u00dfe 44, Charlottenburg, which this archive has listed for a week as a Leibholz Stolperstein \u201cbelonging to another family\u201d. HANS LEIBHOLZ, born 25 January 1912 AT PREUSSISCH FRIEDLAND, KREIS SCHLOCHAU, deported from Berlin on 12 January 1943 and dead at Auschwitz on 22 February; his mother ELSE N\u00c9E ROTHENBERG, born at SCHLOCHAU; his sister LISELOTT, born at Preussisch Friedland; and a second Rothenberg woman of 1884, LAURA, in the same flat. KREIS SCHLOCHAU IS HAMMERSTEIN\u2019S DISTRICT, and Hammerstein is where SALLY LEIBHOLZ \u2014 Jacob\u2019s father, Derrick\u2019s great-grandfather \u2014 was born on 7 July 1847. Every other Berlin household placed so far is Schubin, Stolp or Berlin; this is the first from our own Kreis. A district is not a family and this archive has been burnt twice by good fits, so it is not claimed as kinship. What it does is name the place to write to, and nobody here has ever asked Poland for a Hammerstein record.', 'doc'],
  ['Proposed writing to Poland for a letter that was answered six days ago', 'The Charlottenburg household led to Kreis Schlochau, and Kreis Schlochau is where Sally Leibholz was born, so a row was written saying nobody here had ever asked Poland for a Hammerstein record and it was time to. SOMEBODY HAD. A letter went to Archiwum Pa\u0144stwowe w Koszalinie on 14 SEPTEMBER asking for exactly those two records \u2014 Sally\u2019s birth of 1847 and Georg\u2019s of 1884 \u2014 and the synagogue books. It was answered on 16 SEPTEMBER, reference O-S.6342.406.2026, signed by the Head of the S\u0142upsk Branch: no Standesamt Hammerstein registers for 1874 to 1899, no synagogue-community books for Czarne at all, and no information about where either went. It was acknowledged on the 17th. What the morning\u2019s work adds is one line of corroboration: the public finding aid holds fond 27/364/0, Standesamt Hammerstein [Czarne], at Koszalin\u2019s S\u0142upsk branch, 127 digitised units, and its own date facet reads 1900+ : 128, 1800\u20131899 : 0. The branch\u2019s written negative is confirmed by its own catalogue. That is worth a line and it is not worth a row. SIX TIMES IN ONE DAY NOW, and the cause has not changed: a row or a paragraph written off the back of a fresh find without first reading the file that already answers it. The rule that follows is one command \u2014 grep letters.json and requests/ for the place name before proposing to write to anybody.', 'disp'],
  ['The surname that cannot be searched in a newspaper', 'Priority three of the Findmypast subscription is a newspaper sweep of the Hitchin and Letchworth titles for Wear and Castle notices. It was tried, and the instrument will not do it. WEAR IS ONE OF THE COMMONEST WORDS IN BRITISH NEWSPAPER ADVERTISING, and unlike the census sets the newspaper name box is an OCR string match rather than an index of transcribed names. Wear with the keyword Letchworth returns 9,442 articles headed \u201cMEN\u2019S WEAR AT PUGH\u2019S, Letchworth\u201d; \u201cMartha Maud Wear\u201d returns 285 headed by a knitting joke and a Yorkshire sock advertisement. Two further properties of the tool go down beside the rule that its marriage index silently returns zero when year-filtered: the EXACT-KEYWORD switch does not enforce a phrase, so \u201cPix Road\u201d with it ticked returns 85,719 articles \u2014 and the results URL ignores the county and date parameters, so this one set cannot be swept by constructing URLs the way every other set can. A query with all five filters returned twenty million, more than the unfiltered one. The sweep is re-scoped rather than abandoned: it has to be driven by strings that are not ordinary English \u2014 Shenton, Giddins, Wallis with Baldock \u2014 and by the ship names, Windsor Castle in March 1937 and Dunvegan Castle in August 1939, looking for a departure notice. The surname on its own is not worth trying again.', 'inf'],
  ['The broken year filter is not only the marriages', 'On 16 September this archive proved that Findmypast\u2019s marriage index SILENTLY RETURNS ZERO when year-filtered, using a positive control \u2014 James Shardalow Mort, married at Yarmouth in 1885, who disappears when the search is narrowed to 1885 give or take two. The rule written then named the marriage index. Looking for the six Hitchin Wear children in HERTFORDSHIRE BAPTISMS found the same defect in a second set. Lastname Smith, no year: 15,122 baptisms. The same search narrowed to 1880 give or take five: ZERO. Fifteen thousand Hertfordshire Smiths and not one of them baptised between 1875 and 1885. The 1920 window also returns zero, and without the 1880 control that would have been read as \u201cthe set does not reach the twentieth century\u201d and written down as a coverage bound. THE RULE IS RESTATED WIDER: never year-filter a Findmypast search in any set, and if one is run, run a positive control in the same set in the same breath. The failure mode is the dangerous one \u2014 a clean, plausible zero instead of an error. The children themselves are not there: eleven Wear baptisms in the whole county set, the latest 1891, the only Hitchin one 1786 \u2014 and a whole-category search for Cynthia Y E Wear, born Hitchin 1931, returns her GRO birth entry and no parish baptism anywhere.', 'disp'],
  ['A user tree\u2019s date, confirmed to the day by a government index', 'Priority seven of the Findmypast subscription is probate, and it paid. GEORGE WILLIAM CASTLE the younger \u2014 Martha Maud\u2019s elder brother \u2014 has been carried here on a date taken from a match holder\u2019s tree and nothing else: died 23 August 1939. The England & Wales Government Probate Death Index has him: DEATH DATE 23 AUG 1939, RESIDENCE TOWN LETCHWORTH, probate granted at the London registry on 2 November 1939, linked person CYRIL GEORGE CASTLE. Two things come with it. He was in LETCHWORTH \u2014 the Wears\u2019 town, and the youngest Castle brother was at 9 Boscombe Place, Letchworth in the 1921 census; the Castles had moved from Baldock and the Wears went to the same place. And the dates sit thirteen days apart: Martha Maud sailed from London on the Dunvegan Castle on 10 August 1939, and her brother died at Letchworth on the 23rd. She had been gone a fortnight. Both halves are documented \u2014 BT27 on one side, the probate index on the other. CYRIL GEORGE CASTLE is a new name and is not the ninth child, Ernest Albert Cyril Castle of Baldock; likeliest a son of the deceased, and not asserted. The original calendar page would give the street, the occupation, the executors and the estate value, and it could not be read: Findmypast\u2019s image gateway is session-locked and returns 403 to anything outside the signed-in browser.', 'doc'],
  ['Line 1039, and he was not taken from the family flat', 'This archive has had Jacob Leibholz\u2019s deportation date since the Gedenkbuch was checked on day two. What it never had was the paperwork, and the paperwork moves him to a different street. Wave 44, the 30th Osttransport to Auschwitz of 26 February 1943, page 153, line 1039: \u201cLeibholz \u00b7 Jakob Israel \u00b7 8.8.84 \u00b7 Schubin \u00b7 Beruf OHNE \u00b7 verh. ja \u00b7 Alter 58 \u00b7 arbeitsf\u00e4hig ja \u00b7 Wohnung TREUCHTLINGERSTR. 4 \u00b7 Kennkarte A492552 \u00b7 Kennzeichen 31822.\u201d An AJDC index card repeats the address and adds one word: b/SPIER \u2014 bei Spier, care of a family called Spier. The same page of the list carries \u201c49 b/Grindel\u201d, \u201cb/Gottfeld\u201d and \u201cb/Neumann\u201d against other names, so the convention is not a guess: he was lodging in somebody else\u2019s flat, as many of the 1,100 were. THE 1939 CENSUS HAS HIM AT MERANER STRASSE 2 WITH AUGUSTE AND SIEGFRIED. Between May 1939 and February 1943 he was moved out of his own home, and nothing read here says when or by whom; the word Judenhaus is on neither document and is not used. One column is worth reading twice. In the 1938 directory he is Jaques, Kaufmann, with a clothing firm at Kronenstra\u00dfe 59\u201360. On the list of February 1943 the occupation column says OHNE. And Samuel, re-searched with the consent now ticked, is still not in the Arolsen index at all \u2014 the second test of an absence this archive first recorded on 17 September.', 'doc'],
  ['Two letters on a card, and a house already full', 'The AJDC card for Jacob Leibholz ends \u201cTreuchtlingerstr. 4 b/Spier\u201d. Searching the 1939 census by street and house number instead of by surname turns the two letters into three people: SPIER, MAX, born 12 April 1864, died 26 March 1941; SPIER, HANS, born 2 December 1894; SPIER, ALICE n\u00e9e WOLLSTEIN, born 3 November 1899, the last two both dead before 8 May 1945. And it says what kind of building it was. Treuchtlinger Stra\u00dfe 4 held SEVENTEEN persecuted people from ELEVEN family names on census night \u2014 Br\u00fcck, Cohn, Eisenberg, Eisenberger, Kaphan, Kochanowska, L\u00f6wenheim, Podbielski, Rosenstock, Sundheimer, Spier. Jacob was moved into a house that was already full, three and a half years before the transport. THE SAME SEARCH ON MERANER STRASSE 2 SOLVED SOMETHING ELSE. Fourteen people at the family\u2019s own address, and one of them is ANNA PUFF, BORN 1 JUNE 1895 \u2014 who is one of the eleven names indexed on DocID 11214358, the registration card whose second image nobody here could account for. So that second image is the HOUSE LIST FOR MERANER STRASSE 2, which is exactly what the stamp on the first image says: \u201cLt. Hausliste 1945 n. erfa\u00dft\u201d. One exact match out of seven unplaced names, and the other six would not be in a database of persecuted people if they were not Jewish \u2014 a strong reading, not a proof. It also gives Kurt\u2019s name on that sheet a reason at last: an absent son written against his parents\u2019 flat. That is the THIRD account this archive has given of why he is on that DocID. It is offered as the best one and it is flagged as the third.', 'doc'],
  ['Samuel had a wife, and the census had her all along', 'The address search was then run across every Leibholz address the Berlin directory had given, and the best of them is Kommandantenstra\u00dfe 29 \u2014 Samuel the hat wholesaler\u2019s. It returns two people on consecutive census cards: SAMUEL LEIBHOLZ, born 28.06.1877 in Szubin, \u201cRace\u201d JJJJ, deported to Auschwitz, died 10 April 1943, card VZ153419 \u2014 and FRIDA LEIBBHOLZ N\u00c9E SPREE, born 12 May 1888 in Berlin, \u201cRace\u201d NNNN, no date of death, card VZ153420. SO BOTH BROTHERS WERE IN MIXED MARRIAGES. Jacob married Auguste n\u00e9e Luwinski, NNNN, persecuted \u201ccollectively\u201d; Samuel married Frida n\u00e9e Spree, NNNN. Two men from Schubin, two non-Jewish German wives, and both men deported anyway. Neither wife has a death date. A Mischehe delayed the two brothers and it did not save them. Samuel is not in the Arolsen index at all, searched twice, the second time with the consent ticked \u2014 so the gap there is Arolsen\u2019s. The clerk spelt her surname Leibbholz, with a doubled B. The same sweep separated the two Wilhelms at last: the Weinstra\u00dfe 6 Vertreter is born 30.12.1887 and Yad Vashem\u2019s deported Wilhelm 28.12.1890, so they are not one man. AND IT PRODUCED A COINCIDENCE THAT WAS CHECKED AND DID NOT SURVIVE. At Weinstra\u00dfe 6, in the same building as a Leibholz household, lives HENRIETTE ROSENFELD N\u00c9E SCHMUL \u2014 and Schmul is Friederike\u2019s surname, Jacob\u2019s mother, married to Sally Leibholz at Szubin in 1874. Her record was opened inside a minute: born at SWINEM\u00dcNDE in Pomerania, not Schubin, not Posen. The coincidence is real and the connection is not evidenced, and it is written down so the next reader does not spend an hour on it.', 'doc'],
  ['Opened the half of the address books nobody here had opened', 'Every earlier pass at the Berliner Adressb\u00fccher read TEIL I, householders by name. There is a TEIL IV that lists the same people BY STREET, house by house, with the owner first and the tenants beneath \u2014 and it answers the one question Teil I cannot: who else lived in the building. The 1939 volume, printed page 1629, gives Treuchtlinger Stra\u00dfe 4 entire: \u201c4 E EISENBERG, G., vw. Handelsgerichtsrat \u00b7 Bayer, Oberstleutnant \u00b7 Eberhardt, Kaufmann \u00b7 Jakob, Maurer \u00b7 Mannowsky \u00b7 Petras \u00b7 Rosenstock \u00b7 SPIER, H., Handelsvertreter \u00b7 \u2014 M., Vertreter \u00b7 Sundheimer \u00b7 Wendorf\u201d. The E is Eigent\u00fcmer: the house belonged to GERTRUD EISENBERG N\u00c9E HAAKE, born 9 June 1879, a commercial court councillor\u2019s widow, who also lived in it. Spier H. is HANS, born 2 December 1894; the dash line under him is MAX, born 12 April 1864, dead by March 1941 \u2014 the same dash convention that cost this archive four Leibholz households in a day last week. AND THE INSTRUMENT IS THE BIGGER FIND. digital.zlb.de runs a Goobi viewer with a Solr query API: a POST to /viewer/api/v1/index/query/ returns the IMAGE NUMBER of every page in a volume containing a word, and /pages/{n}/text/ returns that page\u2019s OCR. Three limits were measured and written down. Fraktur OCR mangles words \u2014 \u201cTreuchtlinger\u201d returns nothing in Teil IV and the fuzzy form \u201cTreuchtlinger~1\u201d returns exactly the two right pages. The Teil IV OCR is column-interleaved and reads as nonsense, so it finds a page and cannot read one. And the table of contents gives PRINTED page numbers, not image numbers: printed 1629 is image 5933, an offset of about 4,304 that differs per volume. The whole lookup took six requests.', 'doc'],
  ['Went looking for corroboration, got one match and six misses, and kept the reading anyway', 'Teil IV was then turned on the family\u2019s own address. Meraner Stra\u00dfe 2, 1939, printed page 1598: the owner is SALOMONSKI, M., living away at Tauentzienstra\u00dfe in W 50, and the householders under him are Binder, Briliant, D\u00fcbe, Dunje, Gadischle & Kaulmann, Hesler, Helm, Kamnitzer, v. Koch, *** LEIBHOLZ, J., KFM. ***, Neumann, Pehser, Putzar-Ehrenberg, Schade twice, Wesse. Jacob is listed at his own address as a KAUFMANN \u2014 the trade the transport list of 1943 would record as OHNE. AND IT WAS A TEST, NOT A VICTORY LAP. This morning this archive decided that the unexplained second image of DocID 11214358 is the HOUSE LIST for Meraner Stra\u00dfe 2, on the strength of Anna Puff appearing both on it and in the 1939 census at that address, plus the stamp \u201cLt. Hausliste 1945\u201d. The directory was checked against the sheet\u2019s other six names \u2014 Schmidt, Heinicke, Herzog, Biere, Koch, Nicksch \u2014 AND NOT ONE OF THEM IS A HOUSEHOLDER THERE IN 1939. The reading survives for two ordinary reasons and they are written down as an argument rather than a fact: the directory lists householders only, so lodgers never appear in it \u2014 Anna Puff herself does not \u2014 and a list stamped 1945 in a city where the bombed-out were rehoused constantly should not match a 1939 householder list. It is now a reading with a test behind it instead of a reading with one match behind it.', 'inf'],
  ['Three Luwinskis in post-war Berlin, with trades, addresses and a telephone number', 'The Solr API found this morning searches forty volumes in one request, so the POST-WAR Berlin books were swept by surname for the first time \u2014 27 Namenteil volumes 1949\u20131970 and 13 telephone books 1969\u20131980, all set in Antiqua, so the text layer reads cleanly and no page images are needed. LUWINSKI returns ten pages in eight volumes and three men, every one already in the family tree and every one dead. MANFRED, \u201cSchlosser\u201d and then MODELLSCHLOSSER, Tegel, Egellsstra\u00dfe 16, from 1957 to 1970 \u2014 and Modellschlosser is MODEL FITTER, the exact trade on the 1965 ICEM emigration roll that flew him to Melbourne care of a Mr H. Marquardt, and the tree holds Helga Erna Marquardt n\u00e9e Luwinski. Three sources, one man. JOACHIM, OFENSETZER \u2014 a stove-fitter \u2014 of Ramlerstra\u00dfe 15, Wedding, in 1967 and 1970: a trade and an address the tree does not have for him at all. And KURT, a post-office worker at Stephanstra\u00dfe 7 in 1964 and Peter-Vischer-Stra\u00dfe 25 in 1967, who by the 1970\u201371 telephone book is \u201cLuwinski Kurt \u00b7 4 12 25 12 \u00b7 Beamter \u00b7 52 Waldstr. 60\u201d. THAT KURT IS NOT DERRICK\u2019S FATHER, who left Germany in 1933 and died in Johannesburg in 1969 and cannot be in a 1970 Berlin telephone book. It is the tree\u2019s other Kurt, Leo\u2019s son, recorded as dying in 1995 with no birth date, no place and no occupation. A surname, a city and an era: a correspondence, not an identification, and written down as one. OSKAR LUWINSKI, the master tailor of Steglitzer Stra\u00dfe in 1933, is in none of the forty volumes.', 'doc'],
  ['A candidate in one year, and in no other', 'The same sweep did the other half of row 179. \u201cFricke Auguste\u201d across all forty post-war volumes returns THREE pages and they are TWO WOMEN. The 1957 Namenteil has \u201cFricke Auguste Rentn Chlb 1 Kais-Friedr-Str 79\u201d, a pensioner in Charlottenburg \u2014 the candidate for Kurt\u2019s mother under her last married name. The 1963 and 1970 street sections have \u201cFricke Auguste Putzfrau\u201d in the same building both years, among Fr\u00f6hlich, Kaldenbach, Luckow and Stinka. A working cleaner in 1963 is not the seventy-year-old pensioner of 1957. AND THE PENSIONER APPEARS IN NO OTHER VOLUME OF THE RUN, AND IN NO TELEPHONE BOOK. Four Fricke blocks were opened by hand as well \u2014 1952, 1954, 1963, 1970 \u2014 and none holds an Auguste under her own name or on the dash. If she is Lizzy, she was in Berlin in 1957 and gone by 1959 \u2014 and she was still in Berlin TWO YEARS AFTER HER MOTHER DIED AT ZEUTERN in October 1955. She did not go to Baden to nurse her mother. She went afterwards, and died at Kraichtal, the same town, in 1975. The identification is still unproved and nothing here changes that: one line, no age, no birth date, no maiden name. What the sweep buys is the difference between \u201cthere is a candidate in 1957\u201d and \u201cthere is a candidate in 1957 and in no other year\u201d.', 'inf'],
  ['Asked the opposite question of the Berlin directories, and Heinrich\u2019s widow answered it', 'Every pass at these books has tracked the surname DISAPPEARING \u2014 seventeen Leibholz householders in 1938, six in 1942, every name carrying Israel gone. Nobody here had asked WHO WAS STILL THERE AFTERWARDS. The post-war run is forty volumes to 1980 and set in Antiqua, so it searches outright. LEIBHOLZ returns nineteen pages in seventeen volumes and six names across thirty years. AGNES IS THE ONE THAT CLOSES A LOOP. \u201cLeibholz Agnes, Rentn., SW 61 Mehringdamm 109\u201d in 1957 and 1959, and still at Mehringdamm 109 in the 1975\u201376 telephone book \u2014 nineteen years at one address, with a line of her own by the end. Heinrich Leibholz is this archive\u2019s control case, the man who proved that vanishing from the directory after 1942 does not mean death, because he died on 9 August 1948; the census gives his wife as AGNES N\u00c9E KERGER, born 1889. And SOPHIE LEIBHOLZ, filling in a relief questionnaire in SHANGHAI IN 1946, named him with \u201cwife Agnes\u201d and asked where he was. He had survived; he died two years later; his widow lived on at Mehringdamm for another quarter-century. The others are Ella the nurse at Dominicusstra\u00dfe and then Willmanndamm, Charlotte at Hasenheide, Gerhard a doctor of economics in Steglitz \u2014 not the constitutional lawyer of the same name, who died at G\u00f6ttingen \u2014 Felix at Wiesbadener Stra\u00dfe 57 and then Hilde in the same flat, and Erna alone in Spandau in 1980. NOT ONE IS DOCUMENTED AS BELONGING TO THE SCHUBIN FAMILY, and that is the point: the survivors of this surname in Berlin are the OTHER Leibholz families. Kurt\u2019s people were in Johannesburg, in Potsdam, and in a grave at Auschwitz. One absence was looked for on purpose \u2014 Frida Leibbholz n\u00e9e Spree, Samuel\u2019s non-Jewish widow with no death date, is in none of the forty volumes. Telephone numbers were deliberately not transcribed.', 'doc'],
  ['The Geneteka zero was a one-character bug, and the control had been right all along', 'On 20 September this archive searched Geneteka, the Polish Genealogical Society\u2019s index, for Leibholz marriages in kujawsko-pomorskie and got nothing \u2014 then ran KOWALSKI as a control, got nothing again, and correctly refused to publish the negative. The cause is now found and it is one character. GENETEKA\u2019S PROVINCE CODES ARE POSITIONAL: 01ds dolno\u015bl\u0105skie, 02kp KUJAWSKO-POMORSKIE, 03lb lubelskie, 04ls LUBUSKIE. The query used w=04kp, which does not exist, so the select fell back silently to dolno\u015bl\u0105skie and every search ran against the wrong province. There is a second trap in the same site: the results table is filled by AJAX after the page loads, so a plain fetch of the search URL returns the shell with an empty table, which reads exactly like a zero. WITH w=02kp THE CONTROL PASSES \u2014 Kowalski returns thousands from 1621 on. And the real answers arrive. LEIBHOLZ: zero in kujawsko-pomorskie and zero in wielkopolskie, births and marriages alike. SCHMUL: eighteen marriages in wielkopolskie, fuzzy across Szmul, Szmi\u0142, Schmul, Szmiel and Szmielewski \u2014 Lw\u00f3wek, Przedecz, Pyzdry, Turek, D\u0105bie, Krotoszyn, Gniezno, Pniewy, Ko\u0142o \u2014 and NOT ONE AT SZUBIN. AND THERE IS A STRUCTURAL REASON NONE COULD BE. Szubin is in Geneteka \u2014 parish 13066, indexed from 1715 \u2014 but it is the CATHOLIC parish. Geneteka marks Jewish registers \u201c(moj\u017c.)\u201d, and kujawsko-pomorskie contains NO (moj\u017c.) parish at all, not one across 358 entries. So Geneteka cannot answer the Szubin question and never could. Sally Leibholz\u2019s marriage to Friederike Schmul on 24 November 1874 needs the letter to Bydgoszcz, which is drafted and unsent \u2014 and that is now a tested statement instead of an assumption.', 'disp'],
  ['The register this archive has been writing letters about is online', 'Geneteka having been proved structurally incapable of holding a Jewish Szubin record, the question became where the 1874 register actually is. It is in three places and one of them needs no letter at all. ARCHIWUM PA\u0143STWOWE W BYDGOSZCZY holds the Szubin civil registers in three fonds \u2014 6/1792/0 SZUBIN MIASTO, 1874\u20131935, with marriages from 1874 and, crucially, SKOROWIDZE, alphabetical indexes, FOR 1874\u20131878; 6/1795/0 Wie\u015b I; 6/1796/0 Wie\u015b II. AND FAMILYSEARCH FILMED THEM FROM BYDGOSZCZ. Catalogue koha:402152, Standesamt I, film note \u201cGeburten, Heiraten, Tote 1874\u20131876\u201d, DIGITAL FILM 007998644, 1,121 images. Catalogue koha:402191, Standesamt II, 1874\u20131875, DIGITAL FILM 008016003, 1,160 images. Both open in the viewer with no restriction and no affiliate-library wall. Neither is in the full-text index \u2014 the service\u2019s own groupNumber lookup returns an empty id list for both \u2014 so they cannot be searched by surname and have to be browsed. That is tractable rather than hopeless: Prussian civil registration began on 1 OCTOBER 1874, so the 1874 volumes cover three months, the marriage book should hold a few dozen entries, and the skorowidz should be a single page. A marriage register of 1874 names THE PARENTS OF BOTH SPOUSES. That is the generation above Sally Leibholz and Friederike Schmul, and nobody in this family has ever had it. Request 30 to Bydgoszcz has been updated to cite the three fond references, so a busy office does not have to find them \u2014 and if the browse succeeds the letter is only needed for a certified copy, if at all.', 'doc'],
  ['WITHDRAWN: an image count is not an image', 'An hour ago this archive published that the Szubin civil registers of 1874 are filmed and that \u201cboth are online and neither is restricted \u2014 no permission wall, no affiliate-library restriction, no login beyond the ordinary free account\u201d. THAT IS WRONG AND IT IS WITHDRAWN. The claim was made from the viewer reporting a page count \u2014 1,121 images for film 007998644, 1,160 for 008016003 \u2014 WITHOUT LOOKING AT THE PAGE, because the browser pane was hidden and only the text layer was being read. With the pane displayed, EVERY THUMBNAIL IN THE FILMSTRIP CARRIES A PADLOCK, and opening one returns FamilySearch\u2019s own notice: \u201cIMAGE RESTRICTED. Image access is typically determined by local laws or the custodian who has the original document. To view these images do one of the following: ACCESS THE SITE AT A FAMILYSEARCH CENTER.\u201d THE LESSON IS GENERAL AND GOES BESIDE THE OTHERS. FamilySearch serves a film\u2019s LENGTH to everybody, restricted or not. The padlocks exist only in the rendered page. A film\u2019s availability cannot be read from its metadata, and reading a count as an answer is the same mistake as reading a dead hostname as a dead service. WHAT SURVIVES IS MOST OF IT: the registers exist, they are at Archiwum Pa\u0144stwowe w Bydgoszczy in three named fonds, the town fond has SKOROWIDZE \u2014 alphabetical indexes \u2014 for 1874\u20131878, and the films are 007998644 and 008016003. Only the viewing is blocked, and by the CUSTODIAN rather than by FamilySearch. AND IT LEAVES A FREE ROUTE THIS ARCHIVE CANNOT TAKE ITSELF. The restriction lifts inside a FamilySearch Centre or affiliate library, of which there are several in Brisbane, and access there costs nothing. Film 007998644, the 1874 skorowidz, the marriage of Sally Leibholz and Friederike Schmul on 24 November 1874 \u2014 an afternoon at a terminal. Request 30 to Bydgoszcz is therefore necessary after all, and it is a better letter for citing the three fond references.', 'disp'],
  ['Checked the Polish side too, and the register is unreachable from any desk', 'Having got the FamilySearch answer wrong once, the rest was checked properly. Film 008016003, Standesamt II, is padlocked as well \u2014 so the restriction is on the Szubin material, not on one reel. And the Polish State Archives\u2019 own portal has no scans of it either: szukajwarchiwach returns the three USC Szubin fonds AT FOND LEVEL ONLY, with no unit rows, no inventory and no scan counts, across four differently-worded searches. THE CONTROL IS WHAT MAKES THAT A FINDING RATHER THAN A SHRUG. The same search box returns 127 UNIT-LEVEL ROWS, each with a \u201cNumber of scans\u201d line, for \u201cStandesamt Hammerstein\u201d at Koszalin\u2019s S\u0142upsk branch. The portal does show units and does show scans when they exist. It has neither for Szubin. So the position is now fully mapped: THE REGISTERS EXIST AND NOBODY CAN SEE THEM FROM A DESK. Not digitised in Poland; filmed by the Mormons and locked to a FamilySearch Centre. Two routes remain and both need a person in a room \u2014 a free terminal at a FamilySearch Centre in Brisbane, or the letter to Bydgoszcz, drafted and unsent.', 'inf'],
  ['The town\u2019s paper stops twenty years before the family left', 'The newspaper sweep was re-run the way the re-scoping said \u2014 drive the FILTER PANEL, and search strings that are not ordinary English. First, why the panel works now: THE BROWSER PANE HAD BEEN HIDDEN, and a click cannot reach a pane that is not compositing frames, so every earlier attempt to open a facet failed with the panel apparently inert. Displaying it fixed the problem instantly \u2014 and the same hidden pane is what produced the Szubin padlock error an hour later. Twice in one day, one cause. THE SHIP WAS THE BEST IDEA AND IT CAME BACK EMPTY. Keyword \u201cDunvegan Castle\u201d, 47,783 articles, faceted to Hertfordshire: eleven. 1890, 1897 twice, 1898, 1900, 1901 twice, 1902 \u2014 then 1982 twice and 1986. Mrs Louis Botha landing at Southampton, and coach tours to Skye. NOT ONE IN 1939. AND THEN THE COVERAGE WAS CHECKED, WHICH SHOULD HAVE COME FIRST. LETCHWORTH HAS EXACTLY ONE DIGITISED TITLE: Citizen (Letchworth), 1906\u20131917, 587 issues. The Wears lived at 72 Pix Road and left in 1937\u201339. The town\u2019s only digitised paper stops twenty years before they went. Hitchin has exactly one, the Hertfordshire Express, 1859~1956 \u2014 and 1,403 issues across ninety-seven years is fourteen a year for a weekly, a quarter-sampled run at best. So the priority closes rather than being re-scoped again. A departure notice for this family is not findable here, and the reason is neither the search nor the surname: the paper was never digitised for the years that mattered. That is a coverage bound and it is the end of the route.', 'inf'],
  ['Sauerbaum is Zerbu\u0144, and one spelling is not a search', 'Two of the twenty were done at once, and the second was rescued by David. FIRST: SAUERBAUM, KREIS R\u00d6SSEL, IS ZERBU\u0143 \u2014 today in Gmina Jeziorany, powiat olszty\u0144ski. Its parish and civil district were B\u00d6SSAU: GRO\u00df B\u00d6SSAU is BIESOWO, and the Amtsbezirk B\u00f6ssau was formed on 9 July 1874 with its seat there, so the STANDESAMT B\u00d6SSAU is where Auguste\u2019s birth of 28 August 1893 must be registered. Every Polish archive and portal is organised on the current name, and this archive has spent weeks searching a German one. SECOND: Geneteka\u2019s East Prussian province, never searched until today. The first pass ran ONE spelling, found one woman in one village, and wrote \u201cnothing else in the entire province\u201d. David then said to try every spelling. THE INSTRUCTION CHANGED THE RESULT. LOWINSKI returns THREE BIRTHS AT STANDESAMT R\u00d6SSEL \u2014 Franz 1876, Albert 1881, Bernhard 1884 \u2014 and Kreis R\u00f6ssel is the district Sauerbaum is in, where Johanna Thimm was born in 1871 and Auguste in 1893. LUWENSKI returns the best lead of the day: entry ELEVEN of the new Allenstein civil registry, 1874 \u2014 MARTHA LUWENSKI, FATHER JOSEPH, MOTHER ELISABETH BILITEWSKI. Joachim Luwinski was born at Allenstein in 1865, and the family tree already holds a Joseph Luwinski with no dates, inside the hundred-name Polish block this archive has always called a surname index rather than a descent. IT IS THE FIRST TIME THAT BLOCK HAS TOUCHED A RECORD. The same registry spells one woman LOWINSKI in four years and LUWINSKI in two, which is the whole argument in one line. Neither find is claimed as kinship. Both name exactly which entries Olszty\u0144 should be asked for. AND THE RULE GOES DOWN: ONE SPELLING IS NOT A SEARCH. Every surname sweep from here runs the archive\u2019s whole documented list of variants, one at a time, exact.', 'doc'],
  ['The other half of the emigration roll, in Canberra', 'The National Archives of Australia is free, name-indexed and had never been searched. It holds NAA: A2559, 1965/86/85-88 — the same roll number and the same four line numbers as the Arolsen sheet, BGO 65/135 lines 85 to 88, filed on opposite sides of the world by two archives that do not know of one another. Nine folios, paper, Canberra, not digitised.', 'doc'],
  ['Published that they flew, and the Australian title says they sailed', 'This archive announced this morning that Manfred Luwinski flew from Berlin to Melbourne in 1965. The NAA catalogue entry for the same document reads “arrived per ship FLAVIA ex Bremerhaven/Germany 17 September 1965”. The error was reading the mode of travel off the Arolsen FOLDER heading, “TRANSPORT BY AIRPLANE”, rather than off the sheet. A folder heading describes the folder. And the ship changes the story: Bremerhaven is a four-week sailing from Melbourne, so they left Germany in August.', 'disp'],
  ['A Berlin-born Leibholz who reached Melbourne ten weeks before the war', 'Joachim Peter Leibholz, born Berlin 20 August 1903, landed from the ANCHISES on 12 June 1939 with his wife and a child the catalogue does not name, and was naturalised at South Yarra on 27 July 1944 with his previous nationality recorded as STATELESS. Six files. One of them — A1478, EPCP63 — is “German property taken over by the Polish Government”, and ours are from Schubin in Posen. Not kin on any evidence held, and kept as a lane of its own.', 'doc'],
  ['LAIBHOL* found a file that LEIBHOL* does not', 'Eight surname wildcards were run at the NAA rather than one. A1444, EPC164 is “German currency bonds - J P LAIBHOLZ” — the same man, spelt differently by his own government on a different form, and invisible to any search that assumes a surname has one shape. The LOWINSK* sweep also turned up Helga Lowinsky, born 14 April 1931, who sailed to Australia from Britain in 1964: the right forename, the wrong year, the wrong country, and exactly the kind of near-miss a careless search would have taken.', 'doc'],
  ['Wrote up Paula Luwinski as a discovery, and the Kurt page already had her — with the correction', 'A gazettes.africa sweep returned “Paula Luwinski, a commercial traveller, Mark Hotel” and this archive wrote it up: Kurt’s widow, on the road in 1972, her own shop by 1975. Both notices were already on the Kurt page. So was the identification. AND SO WAS A PUBLISHED CORRECTION OF THAT EXACT MISREADING — the commercial traveller at the Mark Hotel is Phillip Crystal, the man being sequestrated, and Pauline Luwinski is the creditor who brought the application. The archive repeated its own corrected error, from the same snippet, four days later. The standing rule has been widened accordingly: before writing up any find, grep the site for the name.', 'disp'],
  ['What the gazette sweep did add: a second family, and a clean absence', 'Running LOWINSKI, which had never been run, returns eight documents and a separate South African family — Thos. H. Lowinsky in 1922, Mark Lowinsky in 1944 and 1961, Rupert Esmond Lowinsky of Maclear whose estate 671/55 was advertised twice in 1955. They are in print eleven years before Kurt landed, so they are not ours, and they are recorded so that nobody rules them out twice. And LEIBHOLZ, LAIBHOLZ, LEIBHOLTZ and LEIBBHOLZ return NOTHING in any African gazette, in any jurisdiction. Kurt went to Johannesburg alone.', 'doc'],
  ['The compensation files moved on 1 May 2026, and the plan had the wrong addressee', 'The twenty-item list called the Entschädigungsakten the largest unopened seam and put them at the Landesarchiv Berlin in B Rep. 025. They were not there. Until this year they belonged to the ENTSCHÄDIGUNGSBEHÖRDE, a department of LABO — a live authority, not an archive. On 1 May 2026 the Landesarchiv took the whole of it: about 280,000 proceedings and FIFTEEN KILOMETRES OF PAPER, stem files and subsidiary files together, under the federal project Transformation der Wiedergutmachung. The question is four and a half months old, and the archive\'s stated minimum to ask is a name and life dates — which this archive has for Kurt, for Auguste and for Siegfried.', 'doc'],
  ['Ten files a year, scanned free, and a status this archive will not claim', 'The Landesarchiv\'s own page says relatives of Nazi-persecuted people, or those they authorise, may have up to ten files a year digitised at no charge, and that the quota covers Wiedergutmachungs- and Haftakten too. The letter mentions it and does not assert it. Derrick is Kurt\'s son on every document there is, and this archive has published at length the DNA result that puts that paternity in doubt. The German text says so plainly and leaves the decision to them. An archive that has questioned a relationship in public does not quietly rely on it in private.', 'inf'],
  ['A national card index nobody had heard of', 'Named on the Landesarchiv\'s own page: the BUNDESZENTRALKARTEI at the Bezirksregierung Düsseldorf, a central index of compensation claims across the whole Federal Republic. It matters because § 185 BEG gave each western Bundesland its own authority, and this family scattered across three jurisdictions — Kurt to Johannesburg, Auguste to Baden-Württemberg, Siegfried to Potsdam and the far side of the wall. A Berlin-only enquiry can come back empty while the file sits in Stuttgart. The BZK answers the prior question — did they ever file, and where — for all four at once.', 'doc'],
  ['Trove does not work here, and that is a measurement rather than a result', 'Tried again an hour after the first failure. The application loaded; the search box was clicked, the surname typed a character at a time, Return pressed, and the submit button clicked twice — once by coordinate, once by element reference. THE RESULTS AREA NEVER POPULATED AND NO SEARCH REQUEST WAS EVER SENT: the network log holds the configuration calls and the fonts and nothing else, and the console is clean. Trove has not been searched. Not one query has run, and anything said here about what it holds would be invention. The Ryerson Index answered “Server Overloaded” to six attempts in the same sitting.', 'disp'],
  ['Asked Olsztyn for a marriage, not a birth', 'The letter to the Warmia archive is drafted, and it asks first for the marriage of Joachim Luwinski and Johanna Thimm — undated, but bracketed between her birth in 1871 and their daughter\'s in 1893, so somewhere about 1888 to 1893 at Biesowo or Reszel. A marriage record names the parents of both parties. Johanna\'s are known; JOACHIM\'S ARE KNOWN TO NOBODY, and they are the oldest generation reachable on this side. It also names the four indexed entries the spelling sweep found — Allenstein 1874 no. 11, Reszel 1876 no. 68, 1881 no. 31, 1884 no. 9 — so an archivist can go straight to the book. And it gets the 1874 line right: civil registration began on 1 October that year, so the 1865 and 1871 births are church records, and the letter asks whether those books sit at Olsztyn or at the archdiocesan archive.', 'doc'],
  ['Berlin publishes its registry name indexes free, and nobody here had looked', 'The Landesarchiv\'s Standesamtsabfrage lists the alphabetical name indexes to the Berlin birth, marriage and death registers as free PDFs, office by office. A hundred and forty-two offices, harvested from its own autocomplete and all three register types queried for each. THE RESULT IS A BOUNDED JOB: Kurt\'s 1912 birth entry number is in Schöneberg II\'s index for 1911-1913, a single 33 MB file, or in one of twenty single-year 1912 volumes for the old city. And the 1924 marriage — which would name both sets of parents and carry a marginal note if Kurt was legitimised — is in one of two Schöneberg volumes. The PDFs render blank in this browser, so reading one means downloading it, and that is a question for David rather than a decision for the archive.', 'doc'],
  ['Four negatives with one letter in common', 'This was about to be published: “Schöneberg, this family\'s own district, has no online name index at all.” Three queries returned nothing and a sweep of all 142 offices put Schöneberg, Neukölln, Köpenick and Weißensee in the empty column. EVERY NAME WITH AN UMLAUT RETURNED ZERO AND EVERY NAME WITHOUT ONE RETURNED ROWS. The site stores its office names double-encoded — the stored string is literally “Standesamt KÃ¶penick” — and matches on the whole string, so the correct spelling finds nothing and the mangled one finds seventeen volumes. Schöneberg has births from 1874 to 1913 and deaths year by year to 1994. When a set of negatives shares a character, suspect the character.', 'disp'],
  ['Read the 1912 L section at Schöneberg II, and Kurt is not in it', 'Four pages, entries 14 to 1119, every surname from Laband to Lüttke. NO LUWINSKI. Schöneberg I\'s birth indexes stop at 1910 and Schöneberg II\'s volume covers 1911 to 1913, so between them 1912 is covered exactly once and he is not in it. HE WAS NOT BORN IN SCHÖNEBERG. The pages were read by loading the archive\'s own PDF into a renderer inside the browser rather than downloading it — 192 images, only the cover carries a text layer, and the rest is a clerk\'s handwriting photographed from a worn book.', 'doc'],
  ['A Luwinski at number 32 and a Leibholz at number 31', 'This archive held one line — 1913, “LuwinSki, I., Invalide” — with no street, no house and no district, and had never opened it at the page. The page gives CHODOWIECKISTRASSE 32, on the corner of Winsstraße, Berlin N 55, Prenzlauer Berg; the initial is J and not I; and THE HOUSE NEXT DOOR, NUMBER 31, HAS “LEIBHOLZ, G., KFM.” The volume was compiled in the autumn of 1912 and Kurt was born on 11 May 1912. Eleven years later Auguste Luwinski married Jacob Leibholz. Nothing proves the two households are those families and both surnames are borne by other Berliners — but that marriage has never had a geography before, and Kurt\'s birth has never had a district.', 'doc'],
  ['Proved the street instead of reading it off the running head', 'A running head names the first and last street on a page, not the street a column belongs to — and taking a number from one entry and a name from its neighbour is exactly the error that produced the false estate notice in September. So the heading was hunted down on the previous page: “NO. 55 · Chodowieckistr. · A.G. Berl. Mitte — L.G. I”, with the numbers running unbroken from 1 to 40 before the street ends at an arrow marked Greifswalder Str. and a new heading begins. The corner confirms it: 32 is marked “s. a. Winsstr. 42” and 33 “s. a. Winsstr. 33”, consecutive numbers facing each other across a junction. A TEXT-LAYER HIT IS NOT A READ ENTRY: the OCR gave the name and swallowed the street, the number, the neighbour and the initial.', 'inf'],
  ['Standesamt Berlin VIII, 1912, number 963', '*** KURT\'S BIRTH ENTRY, FOUND. *** “Luwinski · Kurt Myszislaus. · 963”, the fourth line of the May block in the L section of P Rep. 523 Nr. 1395, the Landesarchiv\'s name index to the birth register of Standesamt Berlin VIII for 1912. Twenty-one candidate volumes became one in three steps: Schöneberg excluded by reading its entire 1912 L section; the 1913 directory giving Chodowieckistraße 32; and the Berliner Adressbuch\'s own street index, whose FIRST COLUMN IS HEADED STANDESAMT, giving houses 23 to 32 as office 8. Four things then agree — surname, forename, month, and a district arrived at independently — and the arithmetic checks: entry 963 of a register that passes 2561 by December sits 37.6 per cent through it, and 11 May is 36 per cent through the year. Every L page of 1912 was read; there is exactly one Luwinski in the whole year.', 'doc'],
  ['A second forename nobody has ever seen', 'MYSZISLAUS. Not the family tree, not the Arolsen index, not the Berlin registration card, not one South African document has ever carried a second forename for Kurt. It is a German clerk\'s spelling of the Polish Mieczysław — and it is the first thing this archive has learned about him that came from him rather than about him.', 'doc'],
];
---
<Base title="Research log"
  subtitle="What was done, in the order it was done"
  lede="An archive is only as trustworthy as its account of itself. This is the working record — including the leads that went nowhere, which are the ones most worth writing down.">

  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th></th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {log.map(([what, detail, tag]) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{what}</strong></td>
          <td class="small">{detail}</td>
          <td><span class={'tag ' + tag}>{tag === 'doc' ? 'Documented' : tag === 'disp' ? 'Withdrawn' : 'Inferred'}</span></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Where the open work stands</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Line of enquiry</th><th>State</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Arolsen Archives</strong> — the International Tracing Service files</td><td>Confirmed to hold <strong>213 Leibholz person records</strong> and 43 document topics. Not yet enumerated.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Sweep all 5,464 DNA matches</strong> for Leibholz and Luwinski</td><td>Six opened in full, three British and three Portuguese; every one reports no shared ancestral surnames. Strong, not exhaustive.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Henrique's South African trail</strong></td><td>A useful negative — <strong>no Halsey records in the Transvaal depot at all</strong>. His 1962 immigration and 2022 death notice still unfound.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Szubin Jewish registers</strong></td><td>Not yet attempted. Where the family predates Berlin.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Reichsanzeiger expatriation lists</strong></td><td>Digitised and full-text searchable. <strong>39,006 people were denaturalised</strong> across 359 lists, 1933–1945. If the Reich stripped Kurt's citizenship he is in them.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>The next fifteen</h2>
  <p>
    Ordered by expected value against effort, not by how interesting they are. <strong>The first five
    need nobody's permission but the applicant's own, and three of them are already located to a
    document number.</strong>
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Action</th><th>Why it is worth doing</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td><strong>Order estate 12294/2023</strong></td><td>Henrique's <strong>J294 death notice</strong> asks outright for the full names of the father. The likeliest single answer to question 8</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td><strong>Order estate 7584/69</strong> — from the <strong>National Archives</strong>, not the Master <span class="tag disp">Corrected</span></td><td>Kurt's own J294 — what the family said in 1969 about whose son he was. <strong>Corrected the same day:</strong> he died in 1968 and files of that age went to Pretoria long ago. NAAIRS indexes it as <strong>TAB, MHG 7584/69</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td>Send the <strong>Porto <em>óbito</em></strong> request</td><td>Portuguese death records name both parents outright. Drafted months ago, never sent</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td>Send the <strong>1924 Lourenço Marques birth</strong> request</td><td><strong>1924 falls inside</strong> the Maputo archive's 1865–1934 range, unlike Henrique's 1941. Should give parents <em>and</em> grandparents</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>5</strong></td><td><strong>Henrique's 1968 marriage record</strong></td><td>1968 South African registers <em>do</em> carry parents — unlike the 1948 form, which has no parents column at all</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>6</strong></td><td>The <strong>June 1948 ante-nuptial contract</strong></td><td>Found by re-reading column 10 at full resolution. Executed the month Derrick's parents married, registered in the Johannesburg Deeds Office, and not confined to an eleven-column form</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>7</strong></td><td>The <strong>February 1956 contract</strong></td><td>He did it again with Frances. Same office</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>8</strong></td><td>Write to the <strong>closest British match</strong></td><td>Her common ancestors with Derrick are <strong>his own grandparents</strong>, and her shared ancestral place is <strong>South Africa</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>9</strong></td><td>Write to the <strong>10,715-person tree</strong></td><td>Ask whether a cousin of her father's went to South Africa — and about the grandfather her tree records only as <strong>Unknown</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>10</strong></td><td><strong>Big Y-700</strong></td><td>There are <strong>42 branches below R-S11477</strong>. A Big Y places him on one and opens FamilyTreeDNA's Y match list, which MyHeritage does not have</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>11</strong></td><td><strong>Kurt's naturalisation</strong></td><td>The Union gazette published naturalisation lists, giving birth date and place over an official signature. Bears on question 4 — the <em>Israel</em></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>12</strong></td><td>Finish the <strong>gazette sweep</strong></td><td>Oppenheimer, de Witt, Figueiredo, Salkow narrowed to Johannesburg — and when did Kurt let the hat shop go after 1952?</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>13</strong></td><td><strong>Gertrud's death register</strong>, 27 Feb 1943</td><td>She died in the city, not in a camp, on the day of the <em>Fabrikaktion</em>. The entry gives cause of death</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>14</strong></td><td><strong>Auguste and Jacob's divorce file</strong></td><td>Recorded with no date — and in a Berlin <em>Mischehe</em> the date is the whole meaning of the act</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>15</strong></td><td><strong>Szubin and Hammerstein registers</strong></td><td>Settle whether Georg was Jacob's brother or his cousin. Born two months apart in different provinces, which no mother manages</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Six letters, one bounce, and two bad addresses <span class="tag doc">15 September 2026</span></h3>
    <p>
      Sent: the <strong>Arquivo da Defesa Nacional</strong>; the <strong>Arquivo Histórico de
      Moçambique</strong> twice, once for the recruitment records and once for <strong>Ivone's 1924
      birth certificate</strong> — which, unlike Henrique's 1941 birth, falls inside their range; the
      <strong>Arquivo Histórico Militar</strong>; and the <strong>Master of the High Court</strong> for
      the J294 in estate 12294/2023.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>One bounced, and it was my fault.</strong> The Military Archive's published address,
      <code>ahm@mail.exercito.pt</code>, failed with NXDOMAIN. A DNS lookup shows <code>exercito.pt</code>
      has mail servers and <code>mail.exercito.pt</code> has none — the subdomain has been retired, so the
      address that appears on reference pages everywhere was never going to work for anyone. The resend
      went to <code>ahm@exercito.pt</code> and says so in its first paragraph, so the archive learns its
      own contact is broken.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Then it happened again an hour later.</strong> A summary offered
      <code>MastersJohannesburg@justice.gov.za</code> for the Master's office. That office's own contact
      sheet says, in capitals on its first page, that the address <strong>is not in use</strong>. Reading
      the document instead of the summary also produced the thing that mattered: Johannesburg allocates
      estate files to controllers <strong>by the last digit of the file number</strong>. 122<strong>94</strong>
      ends in 4, so the request went to the named controller for 4 rather than into a general inbox.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The corrected address works.</strong> Within nine minutes the Arquivo Histórico Militar
      acknowledged receipt — <em>“Confirma-se a receção da sua correspondência eletrónica a qual será
      tratada pelos nossos serviços. Enviaremos a informação solicitada, caso exista, com a brevidade
      possível.”</em> So the DNS lookup was right and the address printed on the reference pages is
      simply dead.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Their reply also corrects a <strong>third</strong> stale fact from a search summary the same
      morning. The summary placed the archive at <em>Largo dos Caminhos de Ferro</em>; the archive says
      it is at <strong>Largo do Outeirinho da Amendoeira</strong>, that its reading room needs no
      appointment, and that it is open <strong>Monday to Friday, 10:00–12:15 and 13:45–16:45</strong>,
      with document requests and reproductions <strong>10:00–12:00 and 13:45–16:00</strong>.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Check the MX record before sending, and read the source rather than the summary of it.</strong>
      Two lessons, one morning, and both are written down here because an archive that hides its own
      mistakes is worth less than one that does not.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Nine letters out by the end of the morning</h3>
    <p>
      Added to the six already listed: the <strong>Landesarchiv Berlin</strong>, in German, carrying
      three questions at once — Gertrud's death entry of 27 February 1943 and its cause of death,
      the Leibholz–Luwinski marriage of 5 January 1924 <em>and the date of the divorce</em>, and
      <strong>Kurt's own 1912 birth entry</strong>, which may carry a marginal note of
      <em>Vaterschaftsanerkennung</em>. He was born twelve years before his parents married and under
      his mother's name, so that margin is the paper basis of the whole Leibholz descent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Also sent: the <strong>National Archives in Pretoria</strong> for the J294 in estate
      <strong>TAB, MHG 7584/69</strong>, and the <strong>1ª Conservatória do Registo Civil do
      Porto</strong> for Ivone's death record — the latter asking, since her date of death is unknown,
      whether a search by name in the indexes is possible at all.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      Every address was checked for an MX record before sending, which is how three of the day's five
      candidate addresses turned out to be wrong before they cost anything.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Eleven letters, and only one has ever bounced</strong> — the one sent before the checking
      began. Two archives have acknowledged receipt: the <strong>Arquivo Histórico Militar</strong> in
      Lisbon, and the <strong>Archiwum Państwowe w Bydgoszczy</strong>, which adds a useful warning —
      requests are dealt with in order of arrival, <em>complicated ones can take more than a month</em>,
      and anything sent without a full name is not considered at all. It also asks, dryly, that the
      sender check their spam folder while waiting.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Three of the fifteen will not be done by this archive <span class="tag open">Yours to decide</span></h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Items 6 and 7 — the two ante-nuptial contracts.</strong> A South African deeds registry
      <em>“may not give out information acting on a letter or a telephone call.”</em> Copies require
      attendance in person, or a commercial search service that charges for it. So this is not a letter
      anybody can write; it needs a person in South Africa or a paid order, and the archive records that
      rather than pretending otherwise.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Item 5 — Henrique's 1968 marriage record.</strong> Searched on both the groom and the
      bride; FamilySearch's South African marriage collections are patchy after the mid-1960s and it is
      not indexed. It may simply arrive inside the estate file already requested, which is the cheaper
      way to the same fact.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Items 8 and 9 — the two letters to living DNA matches.</strong> Both are written and
      neither has been sent. Everything else this archive has posted went to an institution; these go to
      two private people who did not ask to be contacted, about a finding that touches their family as
      well as this one. They are drafted to open with a narrow question rather than with
      non-paternity, to ask only about the dead, and to say plainly that the DNA and the paperwork
      disagree — so that nobody feels drawn in under false pretences. <strong>And Derrick should
      probably be told before a stranger is.</strong> That is not the archive's call, and it is why
      they are sitting still.
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      <strong>Item 10 — Big Y-700 — is a purchase</strong>, and this archive does not spend other
      people's money.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Parked, and why</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Arolsen Archives.</strong> 213 Leibholz person records are confirmed to exist. The
      declaration of consent carries a criminal-liability clause, and this archive will not tick it on
      anybody's behalf. It needs the family's own signature.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>And one thing that will not appear here at all.</strong> The archive has been told that
      Derrick served on the Mozambique border in the 1970s. He is living; this archive
      <a href={base + '/derrick/'}>names him and publishes nothing else about him</a>, and that includes
      his service. The single fact such a file could hold that <em>would</em> be publishable is a
      next-of-kin address for <strong>Ivone in the 1970s</strong> — and nobody knows where she was
      between the Salkow marriage in 1953 and her death at Porto. But it is his record, it needs his own
      request, and asking him would be faster than asking Pretoria.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Leads closed, with reasons</h2>
  <p>
    These cost time and produced nothing, which is exactly why they are written down — so that
    nobody spends the same time discovering the same nothing.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>FamilySearch for Mozambique.</strong> The catalogue holds 51 items for the country and not one is a vital record. The search cannot succeed.</li>
    <li><strong>Porto passport registers.</strong> The surviving series runs 1951–1991, is described at series level only, and is not digitised. Half a century too late.</li>
    <li><strong>MyHeritage record collections for Ivone.</strong> Nine results, eight Brazilian and one Floridian. No Portuguese or colonial civil registration in them at all.</li>
    <li><strong>Augusto Pereira de Figueiredo</strong> is a candidate and probably a coincidence: no Figueiredo–Mota connection exists anywhere in Aveiro, and his brother emigrated to Brazil like most of that district.</li>
    <li><strong>Halsey in the South African national archives.</strong> Forty documents, not one from the Transvaal depot. Whoever that family was in Johannesburg, they left no estate file in the index.</li>
    <li><strong>Leibholz in the South African gazettes: zero</strong>, across all 54,313 issues — matching the NAAIRS result. The name never entered South African public record at all.</li>
    <li><strong>Colonial Mozambican gazettes</strong> are not held by Gazettes.Africa; its Mozambique run begins at independence in 1975. And <strong>Projecto GERMIL</strong>, the Portuguese military master books, stops in <strong>1908</strong> — thirty years too early for Henrique.</li>
    <li><strong>The 331,829 new Mozambique images at the Lisbon overseas archive.</strong> They are described at file and series level only — <strong>not searchable by personal name</strong>. They exist; they cannot be queried for Ivone. They would have to be read.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sibling archives</h2>
  <p>
    Same method, same author. <strong>The Defranceski</strong> (Istria), <strong>The Lerena</strong>
    (Rosario, and the hinge into this one), <strong>The Falco</strong> (Arienzo). The Lerena archive
    recorded this archive's subject under a wrong given name and a wrong year; it has been corrected to
    <strong>Lynn Derrick Luwinski</strong>, and the two now point at each other.
  </p>
  <p class="small"><a href={base + '/open-questions/'}>The ten questions still open →</a></p>
</Base>
